// Original teaching drafts authored in the coding session. Linked readings are further
// reading, not retained RAG evidence. No provider inference or source-verification score.
export type Lesson = {title:string;concept:string;problem:string;answer:string;task:string}
export type CourseLessons = {context:string;references:{name:string;url:string}[];lessons:Lesson[]}
export const authoredCatalog:Record<string,CourseLessons>={}
const ref=(name:string,url:string)=>({name,url})
const java=ref('Oracle: Java language tutorials','https://dev.java/learn/')
const js=ref('MDN: JavaScript Guide','https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Guide')
const net=ref('IETF RFC 1122: Internet host requirements','https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122')
const ip=ref('Philippine Intellectual Property Code, RA 8293 (read with subsequent amendments)','https://lawphil.net/statutes/repacts/ra1997/ra_8293_1997.html')
const privacy=ref('Philippine Data Privacy Act, RA 10173','https://lawphil.net/statutes/repacts/ra2012/ra_10173_2012.html')
const cloud=ref('NIST SP 800-145: Definition of Cloud Computing','https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/Legacy/SP/nistspecialpublication800-145.pdf')
const wai=ref('W3C Web Accessibility Tutorials','https://www.w3.org/WAI/tutorials/')
const pg=ref('PostgreSQL tutorial','https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/tutorial.html')
const research=ref('HHS: The Belmont Report','https://www.hhs.gov/ohrp/regulations-and-policy/belmont-report/read-the-belmont-report/index.html')
const cyber=ref('NIST: Small Business Cybersecurity Corner','https://www.nist.gov/itl/smallbusinesscyber')
function add(code:string,context:string,references:CourseLessons['references'],rows:string){
 const lessons=rows.trim().split('\n').map(line=>{const fields=line.split('|').map(s=>s.trim());if(fields.length!==5||fields.some(s=>!s))throw new Error(`Invalid authored lesson: ${code}`);const [title,concept,problem,answer,task]=fields;return {title,concept,problem,answer,task}})
 if(lessons.length!==5)throw new Error(`Five lessons required: ${code}`)
 authoredCatalog[code]={context,references,lessons}
}
add("IT103","Quantitative methods turn an operational question into variables, observations, calculations and a qualified decision. A number becomes useful only when its unit, population and collection method are clear. These exercises use invented datasets for instruction, never actual student grades. Keep the dataset, formula and interpretation together so another reader can reproduce the result.",[],`
Course orientation and an evidence contract|A learning contract separates the published course requirements from personal study commitments. Quantitative reasoning requires showing a method and its limits, rather than presenting an unexplained answer. Read the actual approved syllabus for grading weights and policies; this draft does not create new institutional rules.|A report says a learning tool improves performance because four volunteers liked it. What can the report establish?|It establishes those volunteers' reported opinions, not improved performance or a result for every student. A performance claim needs a defined measure, suitable comparison and sampling information.|Prepare a one-page learning contract using the real syllabus. Add an evidence checklist distinguishing observations, calculations and unsupported claims in the volunteer example.
Variables and statistical questions|A quantitative variable records counts or measurements; a qualitative variable records categories. The question determines the useful variable. Statistical thinking anticipates variation, while a single anecdote cannot describe a whole population. Association between variables does not by itself establish a cause.|A help desk records ticket category, waiting minutes and satisfaction from 1 to 5. Classify the variables and propose one answerable question.|Category is nominal, waiting time is quantitative ratio data, and the ordered satisfaction rating is ordinal. Median waiting time by category is answerable; proving that a category causes dissatisfaction needs more evidence.|Create a five-row fictional help-desk dataset and a variable dictionary. Write one descriptive question and explain which causal claim the dataset cannot support.
Sampling and measurement|A population is the full group a question concerns; a sample is the observed subset. Random selection reduces selection bias but does not guarantee representativeness in every sample. Stratification samples within meaningful groups. Nonresponse and ambiguous questions can distort even a well-designed sample.|A college has 120 first-year and 80 second-year learners. Allocate a proportional stratified sample of 20 and explain one limitation.|Allocate 12 places to first year and 8 to second year, selecting randomly within each. The result still needs response tracking; volunteers replacing nonrespondents may introduce bias.|Design a two-stratum survey plan with an operational definition of waiting time. Include consent wording, anonymized fields and a nonresponse record; collect no real names.
Center and dispersion|The mean uses every observation and is sensitive to extreme values. The median is the middle ordered observation and is often more resistant. Range uses only endpoints. Population variance averages squared deviations; sample variance instead divides by n minus one. Always state which formula describes the intended population.|For the complete population of waiting times 2, 4, 4, 6 minutes, calculate mean, median, mode, range and population variance.|Mean 4, median 4, mode 4 and range 4 minutes. Squared deviations total 8, so population variance is 2 square minutes and population standard deviation is the square root of 2 minutes.|Recalculate the measures after replacing 6 with 30. Build a spreadsheet with visible formulas and explain why the median changes less than the mean.
Tables and honest charts|A bar chart compares categories, whereas a histogram groups numerical observations into intervals. Consistent interval boundaries prevent double counting. A graph needs a labelled measure, unit, population and period. Truncated axes, unequal widths and omitted denominators can exaggerate an otherwise correct count.|Times 1, 2, 2, 3, 7, 8 use intervals 0 to less than 3, 3 to less than 6, and 6 to less than 9. Find frequencies.|The three frequencies are 3, 1 and 2; the boundary value 3 belongs in the second interval. The counts sum to six, which checks that no observation was omitted.|Create the frequency table and histogram. Produce a second deliberately misleading chart, label it misleading, and annotate the specific distortion before correcting it.
`)
add("IT102","Programming 1 follows the saved syllabus language: Java. A source file describes instructions, the compiler checks and translates them, and the runtime executes the program. Keep a trace of values alongside the code. A successful compilation is evidence of syntax checking; correctness still requires expected outputs, normal inputs and boundary cases.",[java],`
Orientation and the Java execution path|A console application has an entry point, statements and observable output. In a conventional class named Hello, main receives a String array and starts execution. Comments document intent but do not execute. Distinguish a compiler error from a program that runs and calculates the wrong result.|Given public class Hello { public static void main(String[] args) { System.out.println("Hello, class"); } }, what appears on the console?|The output is Hello, class followed by a line break. The source is conventionally saved as Hello.java, compiled with javac Hello.java and run with java Hello when using the standard class-based launch flow.|Create Hello.java, predict its output, then compile and run it. Record one intentional missing-semicolon error and the corrected version without inventing a successful run.
Classes, statements and program traces|A class groups related code; the main method supplies the conventional entry point in this exercise. Sequential statements execute in order unless control flow changes. Procedural decomposition organizes steps; object-oriented design also models state and behavior. Neither a graphical window nor an IDE is required to trace a console application.|Trace int x = 2; x = x + 3; System.out.println(x * 2); and explain why the result is not 8.|x starts at 2, is reassigned to 5, and the printed expression is 10. The second statement changes stored state before the final multiplication.|Write a three-statement console program that prints a labelled sum. Add useful comments and a before/after trace for every assignment; explain a deliberately introduced logic error.
Variables, constants and primitive types|A variable has a declared type and a value. Java int stores whole numbers within a fixed range; double approximates real-valued quantities; boolean stores true or false; char stores one UTF-16 code unit. String is a reference type. A final variable cannot be reassigned after initialization.|Choose types for an item count, a measured temperature, a pass/fail flag and a person's full name.|Use int for a suitably bounded count, double for an approximate temperature, boolean for the flag and String for the full name. A char is unsuitable for an entire name.|Create a typed record of a fictional purchase using variables. Justify each type, include one final constant and document an input that the chosen type cannot safely represent.
Arithmetic, precedence and conversion|Integer division discards the fractional part in Java. Numeric promotion affects the operation before an assignment stores the result. A cast can lose information; it is not a substitute for validating a value. Parentheses make intended grouping explicit and can clarify expressions even when precedence already specifies evaluation.|Compare double a = 7 / 2; with double b = 7 / 2.0; and int c = (int) 3.9;.|a is 3.0 because integer division occurs first; b is 3.5 because one operand is double; c is 3 because conversion truncates toward zero.|Build a console average calculator for three whole-number scores. Include a case with a fractional average and explain the difference between integer and floating-point division.
Strings and input validation|String objects provide methods for textual operations. equals compares string contents, while == compares reference identity. Scanner token reads and full-line reads consume input differently; a remaining newline can produce an unexpectedly empty nextLine. Validate whether a numeric token is present before parsing and report invalid input clearly.|Why can new String("IT").equals("IT") be true while reference comparison should not be used to check the same text?|equals checks the character sequence; distinct String objects can contain equal text. Reference identity answers whether two references denote the same object, not whether their contents match.|Write a small name-and-quantity reader using Scanner. Demonstrate valid input, a nonnumeric quantity and a multiword name; explain how token and line consumption are handled.
`)
add("WMAD303","These advanced web-system foundation weeks follow the approved JavaScript outline. Work in a browser console or a local JavaScript runtime using fictional data. Record the runtime and the exact inputs. JavaScript coercion can hide mistakes, so predict values and types before executing expressions and use explicit validation at the boundary.",[js],`
Orientation and the JavaScript runtime|JavaScript executes expressions and statements in a host such as a browser. The language and the browser DOM are different layers. console.log exposes a value for inspection but does not create a web interface. Connect course policies to honest testing, source attribution and responsible handling of browser data.|What is logged by const course = "WMAD"; console.log(course + " 303");, and does the code create a heading?|It logs WMAD 303. A console message does not create a document heading; changing page content requires a host API such as the DOM.|Prepare a course learning contract from the actual syllabus, then write and run a three-line script. Label language features separately from browser APIs.
Bindings, types and explicit conversion|let permits reassignment and const prevents reassignment of a binding. Numbers, strings, booleans, null and undefined have distinct uses. The plus operator can concatenate strings. Convert a validated numeric input deliberately; Number("") becomes zero, so a blank input needs a separate presence check.|Compare "2" + 3, Number("2") + 3 and Number("") in JavaScript.|The results are the string "23", the number 5 and the number 0. Treating a blank field as a genuine measured zero would be a validation error.|Build a quantity-and-price calculator that rejects blank and nonfinite inputs. Include a table of input strings, converted values, types and expected outcomes.
Conditional execution and boundaries|A condition selects a branch. Comparisons should express the actual rule, including equality at thresholds. Strict equality avoids implicit type coercion. A boundary test exercises values immediately below, equal to and above a cutoff. Order overlapping conditions from the intended most specific case.|A discount applies when quantity is at least 10. Predict the result for quantities 9, 10 and 11 under quantity >= 10.|The condition is false, true and true respectively. Replacing >= with > incorrectly excludes exactly 10.|Implement a labelled discount decision with validated quantities. Produce a branch table covering the threshold, invalid input and the no-discount path.
Loops and state invariants|A loop repeats while its continuation rule holds. Initialization, test and update must agree or the loop may skip work or never terminate. An invariant states what is already true at each iteration. Trace counters and accumulators separately; inclusive versus exclusive bounds often causes off-by-one errors.|Trace let sum = 0; for (let i = 1; i <= 3; i++) { sum += i; }.|The successive sums are 1, 3 and 6. After the final update i becomes 4 and the condition fails; the body does not add 4.|Write a loop that totals a fictional list of item quantities. Test an empty list and a single item, and document one intentionally faulty bound before correcting it.
Functions and explicit contracts|A function packages a computation with parameters and a return value. A clear contract states accepted inputs, output meaning and invalid-input behavior. Returning a value differs from printing it. Small pure functions are easier to test because the same accepted inputs yield the same output without changing external state.|For function total(price, count) { return price * count; }, calculate total(12, 3) and explain why console output is not guaranteed.|The function returns 36. It contains no logging statement, so a caller must explicitly display or otherwise use that return value.|Implement and test a subtotal function with a separate display step. Include zero quantity, a fractional price and rejected negative quantities with documented assumptions.
`)
add("IT202","A network connects endpoints through links and agreed protocols. Follow a message from an application through transport, addressing and a local link instead of memorizing isolated acronyms. Use diagrams or an authorized isolated lab. Documentation addresses and fictional traffic keep the exercises reproducible without capturing private campus communications.",[net,ref('Unicode encoding FAQ','https://www.unicode.org/faq/utf_bom.html')],`
Orientation and network components|An endpoint sends or receives data; a switch forwards local frames; a router forwards packets between IP networks. A server supplies an application service and is still an endpoint. Physical topology describes connections, while logical topology describes traffic relationships. Read the actual course policies before arranging practical work.|A laptop reaches a server on another network through a switch and router. Identify the endpoint roles and the device crossing the network boundary.|The laptop and server are endpoints. The switch provides local connectivity; the router forwards between networks. A device may implement several roles, so role labels describe behavior rather than its brand.|Draw and annotate a small-office network with two clients, one service, a switch and router. Add a learning contract based on the actual syllabus and identify allowed lab actions.
Bits, characters and transmission|A bit has two values; eight bits form a byte. Character encoding maps text to code units or bytes, so a character is not always one byte. Transmission rate measures bits per second, while file size commonly uses bytes. Protocol overhead reduces usable application throughput.|Ignoring overhead, how many seconds transmit a 1,000-byte file over a 1,000-bit-per-second link?|The payload contains 8,000 bits, so the ideal time is 8 seconds. Actual transfer can take longer because of headers, waiting and retransmission.|Encode a short ASCII word as eight-bit binary bytes. Compare byte counts with a non-ASCII character in UTF-8, then calculate an ideal transfer time and state the overhead assumption.
Layering and encapsulation|Layering separates responsibilities. Application data is carried by transport, IP and link protocols; each adds information needed for its own scope. The seven-layer OSI model is a teaching reference, not a requirement that every implementation expose seven distinct programs. Diagnose a problem at the layer supported by evidence.|A host has a valid IP address and can reach an IP endpoint, but a name lookup fails. Which service deserves investigation first?|Investigate name resolution rather than concluding that all physical connectivity is broken. Successful IP reachability narrows the problem but does not prove every application or port works.|Draw an encapsulation diagram for a web request and map its functions to OSI and TCP/IP models. Diagnose three fictional failures with the observation that supports each hypothesis.
Ethernet media and local delivery|Ethernet defines wired link behavior and frame delivery. Copper and fiber have different distance, interference and installation properties. Wireless uses different link mechanisms. Choose a medium from requirements and the actual supported equipment; cable appearance alone does not prove category, wiring correctness or negotiated link speed.|Two wired hosts on one LAN can communicate without sending their local frames through the Internet. What device usually forwards between their ports?|A local Ethernet switch usually forwards frames using learned MAC addresses. A router is needed for other IP networks, not every local frame.|Create a labelled Ethernet patching plan for an isolated lab. Record link indicators, negotiated speed and an application check separately; inspect only approved cables and equipment.
MAC, IPv4 and IPv6 addressing|A MAC address supports link-local frame delivery; IP addresses support network-layer identification and routing. IPv4 addresses contain 32 bits and IPv6 addresses contain 128 bits. Address scope matters: a valid-looking address is not automatically reachable from every network. A prefix identifies the network portion.|What are the IPv4 network and ordinary host range for 192.0.2.0/24 in a documentation-only example?|The network is 192.0.2.0, ordinary host addresses are 192.0.2.1 through 192.0.2.254 and the broadcast address is 192.0.2.255. This block is reserved for documentation, not a public deployment.|Annotate MAC and IP fields in a fictional path diagram. Contrast a documentation IPv4 address, an IPv6 link-local address and a globally routable design without probing real hosts.
`)
add("WMAD304","Network management connects topology, addressing, device capability and operating evidence. An attractive diagram is not enough: state which traffic must work, which access must be blocked and what observation would confirm either result. Use documentation ranges and an isolated simulator; never change production switching, routing or firewall settings for an exercise.",[net,ref('IETF RFC 4291: IPv6 addressing','https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc4291')],`
Orientation and an operations baseline|An operations baseline records the intended network, responsible people, permitted changes and success measures. A symptom and a cause are different: a slow page can result from application, name resolution, link or server conditions. Read the real syllabus for local rules, and agree on evidence before diagnosing.|A report says the network is down because one website is slow. What evidence is missing?|It lacks the affected users, time, other destinations, IP/name-resolution checks and application response observations. One slow site does not establish total network failure.|Create a learning contract and a fictional network incident worksheet with scope, baseline, hypotheses, permitted checks and rollback responsibilities.
Ethernet, IPv4 and VLAN review|A VLAN defines a logical broadcast domain at the link layer. Devices in separate IP networks normally need routing to communicate. VLAN separation alone does not authorize inter-VLAN access; an explicit policy and enforcement point are necessary. IP configuration requires compatible prefix, address and intended next hop.|Hosts 192.0.2.10/24 and 198.51.100.10/24 occupy separate lab VLANs. Can a switch-only connection guarantee communication?|No. The design needs routing between their IP networks and permitted policy. A trunk may carry VLAN frames but does not itself perform inter-network routing.|Draw two VLANs with documentation addresses. Specify permitted and denied flows, the router interfaces and the evidence needed to verify each rule in a simulator.
Devices, roles and power budgets|Switches, routers, firewalls, access points and controllers solve different forwarding, policy or management problems. A layer-three switch can route; product categories may overlap. Power over Ethernet also requires a compatible power class and an adequate aggregate budget. A firewall does not replace secure endpoint configuration.|Six access points each need 15 watts and the available PoE budget is 75 watts. Is the budget sufficient before allowing overhead?|No. The stated demand is 90 watts, exceeding the budget by 15 watts. Device compatibility and vendor headroom must also be checked.|Build a device-role and power-budget table for a fictional six-room installation. Identify which claims need a real specification sheet before procurement.
Topology and failure domains|Two-tier, three-tier and spine-leaf architectures organize aggregation and paths differently. Redundant links help only if control protocols and failure handling prevent loops and converge acceptably. Separate on-premise and cloud dependencies in the diagram. Choose a topology from scale, failure tolerance, operations skill and cost.|A branch has one uplink and two access switches. What single failure can isolate both switches from remote services?|Failure of the common uplink or its upstream device can isolate the branch. Two access switches alone do not remove that shared dependency.|Compare a small-office design with a two-tier campus design. Mark single failure points, management paths and the user-visible effect of each chosen failure.
Transport and addressing checks|TCP provides a reliable ordered byte stream; UDP provides datagrams without that transport guarantee. Application needs determine the choice. Prefix length, route and local scope constrain delivery. IPv6 uses unicast, anycast and multicast and does not use IPv4-style broadcast; not every address is suitable as an off-link destination.|For a conventional IPv4 192.0.2.0/26 subnet, identify the next subnet boundary and number of ordinary host addresses.|The next boundary is 192.0.2.64 and the first subnet has 62 ordinary host addresses, .1 through .62. .0 is the network and .63 the broadcast address.|Document a client IP/prefix/gateway/DNS checklist and a /26 address plan. Explain one TCP/UDP choice and why an IPv6 link-local next hop must be associated with a link.
`)
add("IT101","Computing combines representation, processing, storage and communication to support human goals. Evaluate a system by what it does and by its effect on people, rather than by a product label. Every example in this draft is fictional. When using historical or professional readings, distinguish a dated event from an interpretation of its significance.",[privacy,wai],`
Orientation and responsible study|A course plan tells learners the expected outcomes, assessments and working rules. Separate an official policy from a personal study preference. Computing work also requires attribution, careful handling of personal data and truthful reports of what a program or device actually did. A demonstration is not evidence of universal reliability.|A learner labels an unrun design as tested. What evidence should a responsible report contain instead?|It should identify the work as a design, state the proposed test and record real observations only after execution. Honest limitations let another person reproduce the work.|Read the approved syllabus and create a learning contract. Add an evidence log with separate columns for proposed checks, actual observations and unresolved questions.
Computing history and social effects|Computing history includes changes in electronic hardware, stored programs, interfaces and communication. A historical timeline needs dates and attributable sources, while the significance of an event requires argument. Increased processing power does not automatically produce fairer access; cost, language, connectivity and accessibility shape social benefits.|A timeline claims that a smaller device always improves access. What counterexample should be considered?|A smaller device may still be unaffordable or unusable with an inaccessible interface. Physical size is one design characteristic, not a complete measure of inclusion.|Construct a five-event computing timeline using dated readings. For each event state one technical change and one social effect, distinguishing evidence from your interpretation.
Computing careers and ethical decisions|Computing roles include software development, support, networking, information systems and research. Their responsibilities overlap but the evidence each produces differs. Ethical analysis identifies affected people, possible harm, consent and accountability. Technical permission to view data is not the same as a legitimate purpose to disclose it.|A support technician can view a student's record and wants to post it to a class chat. Is access alone sufficient justification?|No. Access for support does not establish permission or a necessary purpose for public sharing. Use minimal relevant information through authorized channels.|Create a role-and-responsibility map for a fictional course portal. Analyze a privacy dilemma, proposing an action and a way to verify that the action reduces exposure.
Computer classifications and system requirements|Computer classifications describe capabilities or use: embedded controllers, personal computers, servers and high-performance systems have different constraints. A server is also a role that software can perform. Choose hardware using workload, memory, reliability, energy and maintenance needs rather than the largest advertised clock speed.|A kiosk needs a simple local interface and low power; a database service needs concurrent access and backups. Should identical purchase criteria decide both?|No. The kiosk emphasizes interaction, durability and energy; the service emphasizes workload capacity, availability, storage and recovery. Both still need security and maintenance.|Compare three fictional workloads against a requirements matrix. Justify a suitable system category and one measurement needed before buying hardware.
Hardware, software and data representation|Processors execute instructions; RAM supports working state; persistent storage retains data across power loss. System software manages resources, while applications serve user tasks. Binary represents values, and encodings assign meaning to bit patterns. A software license determines permitted use rather than technical installability.|Convert binary 1010 to decimal and explain why saving a file differs from leaving it only in RAM.|1010 is 8 plus 2, or decimal 10. A saved file resides on persistent storage; volatile RAM ordinarily loses its contents when power is removed.|Create a component diagram, convert four binary values, and classify an operating system and two applications. Record the license source for an example application without assuming free download means unrestricted reuse.
`)
add("IT201","Data structures specify how values are organized and what operations a program supports. Algorithms describe the steps using those structures. State the input size and count a meaningful operation before comparing efficiency. A trace explains correctness for a case; asymptotic analysis explains growth and still needs assumptions about the representation.",[ref('MIT OpenCourseWare: Introduction to Algorithms','https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-006-introduction-to-algorithms-spring-2020/')],`
Arrays, sets and an evidence contract|An array supports indexed positions; a set models distinct membership. The representation determines costs: array lookup by index differs from searching an unsorted array. A set's uniqueness rule does not guarantee one specific runtime bound for every implementation. Read the course policies before selecting a programming environment.|Insert values 4, 4, 7 into an array and into a mathematical set. What contents result?|The array can retain [4,4,7]; the set contains {4,7}. Removing duplicates changes the meaning when repeated observations matter.|Create a learning contract and compare array and set behavior on a fictional attendance-code list. Preserve duplicate evidence in the source list and explain when deduplication is appropriate.
Linear and binary search|Linear search examines candidates sequentially. Binary search repeatedly halves an ordered search interval and requires ordering consistent with the comparison. Keeping an interval invariant prevents lost candidates. The logarithmic comparison count does not include the cost of initially sorting an unsorted input.|Search the sorted list [2,5,8,12,16,23,38] for 23 using middle positions.|Inspect 12, then 23 under the usual midpoint rule. The first comparison discards the lower half; a linear scan from the start would inspect six elements.|Trace successful and unsuccessful binary searches with low, high and midpoint values. Explain why applying the same procedure to an unsorted list is invalid.
Growth rates and cost models|Big O gives an asymptotic upper bound and does not provide an exact elapsed time. A single full scan is linear; two nested full scans can be quadratic. Constants, data sizes and early exits affect practical performance. State whether analyzing worst, average or best case rather than mixing them.|An algorithm compares every ordered pair among n items with two complete nested loops. How many pair visits occur at n=4 and n=8?|There are n squared visits: 16 and 64. Doubling n quadruples this count, illustrating quadratic growth under the stated loops.|Count operations for a scan and a pairwise comparison on three input sizes. Plot counts, state the cost model and distinguish measured time from the mathematical bound.
Stacks, queues and expression order|A stack removes the most recently added element; a queue removes the earliest waiting element. Their operation order models different requirements. Parentheses and operator precedence determine expression structure. In postfix evaluation, operand order is critical for subtraction and division, even when addition appears forgiving.|Evaluate postfix 8 3 - 2 * using a stack.|Push 8 and 3, subtract 3 from 8 to obtain 5, push 2 and multiply to obtain 10. Reversing the subtraction operands would be incorrect.|Show a full stack trace for two postfix expressions and a queue trace for four service requests. Justify which structure fits undo history and which fits arrival-order processing.
Recursion and repeated subproblems|A recursive algorithm needs a base case and progress toward it. The call stack holds unfinished work. Dynamic programming stores results of overlapping subproblems so they need not be recomputed. Memoization saves repeated work but still consumes memory and requires a correct key for each subproblem.|Using F(0)=0 and F(1)=1, calculate F(5) and explain why naive recursion repeats work.|The sequence is 0,1,1,2,3,5. Separate branches recompute values such as F(3); memoization reuses the stored result for the same argument.|Trace a recursive factorial with a valid base case, then build a table for Fibonacci through F(6). Compare repeated calls with stored subproblems and report stack/memory tradeoffs.
`)
add("IT204","A platform combines hardware, operating-system services and software interfaces. Reason from a workload to the services it needs rather than assuming one operating system is always best. Use a disposable local environment for practical observations. Record commands and outputs accurately and keep real credentials, devices and institutional services outside the exercise.",[ref('Linux kernel documentation','https://docs.kernel.org/'),cloud],`
Orientation and platform layers|Hardware executes instructions, the operating system manages shared resources, and frameworks provide reusable application facilities. A platform decision includes compatibility, maintenance and the people operating it. Read the approved syllabus for rules and requirements; the draft provides no new school policy.|A mobile application uses a runtime and operating-system file API. Which layer ultimately mediates its access to storage?|The operating system mediates storage access using permissions and device services. The runtime/framework can simplify the request but does not remove the underlying resource and security boundaries.|Prepare a course learning contract and draw the hardware, operating-system, runtime and application layers for a fictional kiosk, annotating one dependency at each boundary.
Processes, concurrency and scheduling|A process has execution state and resources; threads share parts of a process. Concurrency permits overlapping progress, while parallelism requires simultaneous execution. A scheduler chooses runnable work under a policy. Two concurrent updates can lose data if the read-modify-write sequence is not coordinated.|Two workers both read a counter at 5 and each writes back 6. What went wrong?|The updates raced, losing one increment; the intended combined result was 7. A protected atomic update or suitable synchronization is needed.|Trace the interleaving of two fictional workers. Compare serial and concurrent outcomes and propose a synchronization boundary without assuming faster hardware fixes the race.
Memory and isolation|Virtual memory gives processes an address-space abstraction; physical memory remains finite. Protection prevents one ordinary process from freely modifying another's memory. Paging can move data between memory and storage, but excessive paging hurts performance. A memory leak retains unreachable or unnecessary allocations over time.|An application grows from 200 MB to 900 MB while repeating the same completed task. Does the observation prove a leak?|It suggests a problem worth measuring, but caches and workload changes can also explain growth. Reproduce the workload and examine retained allocations before concluding.|Build a memory-budget worksheet for three fictional processes and the operating system. Describe observations that distinguish a useful bounded cache from unbounded retention.
Files, devices and protection|A file system gives names and structure to persistent data. Permissions and ownership control access, while device management coordinates hardware requests. Fault tolerance and backups solve different problems: redundant disks do not recover a mistakenly deleted file without a retained copy. Test restoration separately from backup creation.|A mirrored drive faithfully copies an accidental deletion. Why is the mirror not a complete backup strategy?|It preserves availability after some hardware failures but also reflects logical changes. A versioned, separately protected backup can retain the earlier file.|Design a directory-permission matrix and a backup/restore check for a fictional lab. List the expected files and verify recovery in a disposable environment or label the plan unexecuted.
Virtualization, scripts and platform choice|Virtualization provides isolated virtual resources over physical infrastructure; containers share more of the host kernel. Scripts automate repeatable actions but need explicit inputs, safe permissions and error handling. Real-time requirements concern bounded response deadlines, not simply average speed. Choose architecture from the required guarantees.|A temperature alarm must respond within a fixed deadline, while a report may finish later. Which requirement is real-time?|The alarm has a deadline whose violation matters. A fast average report does not establish real-time behavior, and a virtual machine alone does not guarantee deadlines.|Compare a virtual machine, container and embedded controller for three fictional workloads. Create a read-only inventory script and document its output, permissions and failure handling.
`)
add("WMAD306","The approved first-five-week outline connects system integration with information systems, organizational strategy and security. Evaluate a mobile or web solution as part of a working organization: people, information, processes and infrastructure all matter. Use fictional organizations and trace every proposed feature to a requirement and a checkable result.",[cyber,privacy],`
Integration requirements and architecture|Integration joins components so that a business process works end to end. Requirements define observable outcomes; acquisition decides what is built or sourced; deployment introduces a tested change. Architecture identifies responsibilities and interfaces. A project schedule cannot substitute for interface compatibility or a realistic acceptance test.|A booking app and payment service each work alone, but bookings duplicate after a retry. What integration requirement is missing?|The combined flow needs a stable operation identifier and duplicate-handling behavior. Independent component tests did not establish a safe end-to-end retry contract.|Prepare a learning contract, architecture sketch and acceptance checklist for a fictional mobile booking service, including a retry failure and rollback owner.
Information systems and resource management|An information system includes people, processes, data and technology. Transaction-processing systems record operational events; reporting summarizes them for decisions. Data ownership, retention and quality need explicit responsibility. A dashboard is useful only if its underlying events and definitions are trustworthy.|A shop records five sales but a dashboard shows four because one event arrived late. What should the report communicate?|It should disclose the reporting period and freshness, reconcile the delayed event and avoid presenting an incomplete total as final. Operational and reporting views may update at different times.|Map a fictional sales transaction from capture through storage to a management report. Assign responsibility for validation, correction and retention at each stage.
Organizations in the digital economy|Digital channels can reduce coordination cost and expand reach, but also introduce dependency on networks, platforms and user trust. Business pressure should be translated into a measurable requirement. More features alone do not establish competitive advantage; adoption, service quality and operational fit matter.|A local store wants online ordering because a competitor has an app. Which evidence should precede building it?|Investigate customer need, current ordering delays, connectivity, fulfillment capacity and expected cost. A competitor's feature list is a hypothesis source rather than proof of demand.|Write a problem brief for a fictional store, compare two digital responses and define a baseline measure plus a small, reversible pilot.
Strategic systems and decision evidence|A strategic information system supports a chosen organizational capability. Benefits need a baseline, target, owner and time period. Cost includes integration, training, support and exit as well as development. Compare alternatives under the same assumptions and identify uncertainty rather than hiding it inside a precise-looking estimate.|Option A costs 10,000 initially plus 1,000 monthly; B costs 4,000 initially plus 2,000 monthly. Compare a six-month cost.|A totals 16,000 and B totals 16,000 under the stated assumptions. Equal modeled cost does not settle reliability, training, support or switching considerations.|Prepare a six-month decision matrix with cost, usability, availability and data-control criteria. Document weights and a sensitivity case that could change the preferred option.
Ethics, threats and protection|A threat can exploit a weakness to harm confidentiality, integrity or availability. Risk combines consequences with likelihood under stated assumptions. Minimize collected information, restrict access and plan recovery. Consent alone does not replace secure handling; a protective control needs an owner and evidence that it works.|A mobile app stores customer contact data in a publicly readable file. Name the threatened property and a practical first correction.|Confidentiality is directly threatened. Remove public access, restrict authorized use and investigate actual exposure through the responsible response process; merely hiding a link is insufficient.|Create a threat-and-control table for the fictional booking flow. Include unauthorized reads, duplicate writes and service outage, with one verification step for each control.
`)
add("WMADE1","Multimedia combines text, images, sound, video and interaction to communicate a purpose. A successful asset must suit its audience, delivery constraints and rights of use. Work with original or explicitly licensed assets. Separate creative judgment from technical evidence such as dimensions, file size, accessibility and successful playback.",[wai,ip,privacy],`
Orientation and multimedia elements|Choose media by what the learner needs to perceive or do. Raster images use pixels; vector graphics describe shapes. Lossless formats preserve represented data, whereas lossy encoding trades some fidelity for size. Audio, video and animation need accessible alternatives and a clear purpose, not decoration alone.|A diagram must remain sharp at several sizes and a photo must preserve subtle texture. Which broad representations fit?|A vector representation often suits the scalable diagram; a raster representation suits photographic detail. Actual format choice also depends on tool support, transparency, delivery and licensing.|Read the syllabus and make a learning contract, then storyboard a three-screen original explainer. Justify text, image and audio choices and include text alternatives.
Authoring tools and graphics workflow|Authoring tools combine assets and interaction; graphics tools create or edit individual assets. A reproducible workflow keeps editable originals separate from delivery exports. Dimensions, color handling, compression and device capability affect the output. A smaller export is useful only if its meaning and readability survive.|An image is 1200 by 800 pixels with three uncompressed 8-bit color channels. What is the raw pixel storage size before headers?|It contains 960,000 pixels and 2,880,000 bytes of channel data. An encoded file may be smaller or larger depending on compression and metadata.|Produce an original graphic, retain its editable source and export two delivery versions. Compare dimensions, file size and readability, recording the tool and settings used.
Creative roles and design problem solving|A project may involve researchers, writers, designers, illustrators, sound editors, developers and reviewers. A clear brief states audience, purpose, constraints and success criteria. Iteration compares alternatives against that brief rather than personal taste alone. Keep attribution and accessibility responsibilities visible throughout the workflow.|A poster is visually striking but its event date is unreadable on a phone. Which brief requirement should drive revision?|The communication purpose requires the audience to find the date. Improve hierarchy and legibility, then test the intended display size rather than judging only the full-size canvas.|Write a design brief for a fictional campus event. Assign roles, create two layout alternatives and document a peer-review change linked to the brief.
Intellectual property categories|Copyright concerns protected expression; trademarks distinguish commercial source; patents concern qualifying inventions; undisclosed information depends on maintaining secrecy. Owning a file does not automatically grant reuse rights. Check the applicable law, license and permissions; attribution alone is not a universal substitute for permission.|A team finds a photograph online and adds the photographer's name. Does attribution alone establish a right to republish it?|No. The team must identify a license, permission or an applicable legal basis for the intended use. Credit and permission answer different questions.|Create a rights ledger for five fictional assets, distinguishing creator, source, license, intended use and unresolved permissions. Replace an asset when its rights cannot be established.
Digital rights, privacy and ethical publication|A multimedia release can involve both intellectual-property rights and personal information. Permission to use a photograph's copyright does not necessarily settle privacy issues concerning a person depicted. Record the intended audience and reuse limits. Educational purpose does not make every public upload automatically permissible.|A class licenses a portrait from its photographer but the identifiable subject objected to public posting. Which separate issue remains?|The copyright license does not resolve the subject's privacy and consent context. Pause publication and follow the institution's applicable privacy review rather than assuming one permission covers everything.|Audit the fictional explainer's assets and consent assumptions. Create a publication checklist and a revised version using original nonidentifiable material where permissions remain uncertain.
`)
add("WMADE3","Electronic business uses digital information and channels across an organization's activities; e-commerce concerns transactions within that wider setting. Classify a model by how value, revenue and participants interact. Fictional cases allow explicit assumptions without claiming current merchant policy, legal approval or real customer demand.",[privacy,ref('UN E-Government Knowledgebase','https://publicadministration.un.org/egovkb/')],`
Orientation and electronic business|E-business includes procurement, service, coordination and record handling as well as online sales. Benefits such as reach and automation bring costs in fulfillment, access, security and trust. A useful analysis follows the complete process and identifies people excluded by the channel. Read actual course rules for required work.|A cooperative takes orders online but still reconciles inventory and delivery manually. Is every part of its business digital?|No. Its online transaction channel is only one part of a mixed business process. Manual fulfillment and data reconciliation remain important dependencies.|Prepare a learning contract and map a fictional cooperative from order to fulfillment, labelling digital and manual steps, benefits and failure points.
Revenue and intermediary models|A merchant earns from selling its offering; a broker connects parties; advertising sells audience access; subscriptions charge for continued access; information intermediaries organize useful information. A mixed model combines mechanisms. Classifying revenue does not settle whether the service actually creates customer value.|A platform charges a monthly access fee and a separate fee for completed matches between sellers and buyers. How should its model be described?|It combines subscription and brokerage mechanisms. State both rather than forcing one label, and identify which participant pays each fee.|Compare merchant, brokerage, advertising, subscription and information-intermediary models for a fictional local marketplace. Draw money and information flows separately.
E-commerce participant categories|Categories such as B2C, B2B and C2C describe transaction participants, not the software platform's brand. C2B reverses the usual seller direction; government transactions need a clearly stated role. One platform may support several categories. Identify a specific transaction before assigning its category.|A freelance individual sells a design service to a company; the same company buys stock from a supplier. Classify both.|The individual's sale to a company is C2B under this participant classification; the supplier transaction is B2B. Their shared website does not make the categories identical.|Create six fictional transaction cards covering C2C, B2C, C2B, B2B, C2G and B2G. Explain the participant and transaction evidence behind each label.
Transaction journeys and platform fit|A category label is only the start of design. Discovery, agreement, payment, fulfillment, support and dispute handling must fit the participants. Consumer access, supplier integration and public-sector accountability introduce different constraints. Compare required behavior rather than choosing the platform with the longest feature list.|A B2B buyer needs approval before a purchase, while a household buyer pays immediately. What flow difference should the system support?|The B2B flow needs a request/approval state before commitment. The household flow may be immediate, but both need clear price, order status and recovery when payment and fulfillment disagree.|Design parallel B2C and B2B journeys for the same fictional product. Mark authorization, payment failure and fulfillment confirmation, then justify platform requirements.
E-governance service relationships|G2C concerns government services to citizens, G2B services to businesses, G2G coordination between agencies and G2E services to employees. Digital governance also needs transparency, accessible alternatives and responsible information handling. An online form alone does not establish faster or fairer service.|Classify a citizen certificate request, employee leave portal and interagency record verification.|They represent G2C, G2E and G2G respectively in this model. Each still needs authorized access, status visibility and a clear service owner.|Map one fictional G2C and one G2B service, including required data, accessibility, processing status and a measure of service improvement without collecting real identifiers.
`)
add("WMADE4","Cloud computing is a delivery model for computing capabilities, not a synonym for any remote computer. Evaluate resource access, service responsibilities, deployment scope, metering and exit requirements. Paper designs and local simulations are sufficient for these drafts; opening paid accounts or uploading institutional data is not an exercise requirement.",[cloud],`
Orientation and a cloud learning contract|Start from a workload, its information and the people responsible for it. A course contract should distinguish institutional requirements from personal study goals. Cloud experiments need bounded costs, fictional data and a cleanup plan. A provider's convenience does not transfer the institution's responsibility to understand access and retention.|A learner proposes uploading a class list to try a storage feature. What should an educational exercise use instead?|Use fictional records in a bounded local simulation or approved sandbox. A learning objective about storage does not require exposing real personal information.|Read the approved syllabus and prepare a cloud-study contract with objectives, fictional inputs, allowed tools, cost boundaries and evidence to record.
Requirements before provisioning|Cloud planning begins with workload, availability, data sensitivity, performance, support and cost assumptions. A requirements table should state a measurable need and its evidence. Distinguish a feature request from a policy constraint. Document what must be deleted or exported when an experiment ends.|A team requests unlimited storage for a five-file classroom demonstration. What is a better bounded requirement?|Specify the five fictional files, maximum total size, access rules, retention period and successful retrieval check. Unlimited capacity is neither necessary nor a useful acceptance criterion.|Create a cloud-experiment plan for a fictional document service with access rules, quota, deletion criteria and an unexecuted-versus-observed evidence log.
Cloud characteristics and service layers|NIST distinguishes on-demand self-service, network access, resource pooling, elasticity and measured service. IaaS, PaaS and SaaS allocate different operational responsibilities. A service category describes what is supplied, while security and configuration responsibilities must still be checked in the actual agreement.|An organization deploys its application onto a managed runtime while the provider manages the underlying operating system. Which service model best fits?|PaaS is the closest fit under the stated abstraction. The organization still manages its application behavior, identities, data and configuration obligations.|Compare IaaS, PaaS and SaaS for a fictional teaching portal. Produce a responsibility matrix for operating system, application updates, identities, data and recovery.
Architecture, capacity and cost|A cloud design separates compute, storage, networking and identity. Elastic capacity must still be bounded and observed. Measured use can create variable cost; model storage, requests and transfer separately. Availability needs explicit recovery behavior rather than assuming remote infrastructure cannot fail.|A demonstration uses 2 GB at 3 currency units per GB-month and 100 requests at 0.02 each. What is the modeled monthly cost before other charges?|Storage contributes 6 and requests contribute 2, totaling 8 under the stated fictional rates. Transfer, taxes, minimums and additional services are excluded assumptions.|Draw a three-component document-service architecture. Build a fictional cost worksheet for normal and doubled use, mark excluded costs and specify a budget alert and cleanup check.
Public, private, community and hybrid deployment|Deployment models describe who uses and controls the cloud environment. Public cloud serves a broad customer base; private cloud is for one organization; community cloud serves a defined group with shared concerns; hybrid joins distinct environments. Location alone does not determine the model or prove regulatory suitability.|Two hospitals jointly use an environment for their shared requirements, while one also connects its own private environment. How can the models be described?|The shared environment may be community cloud if that governance fits; connecting distinct cloud environments can form a hybrid arrangement. Verify the actual control and portability arrangements.|Compare all four deployment models against a fictional college workload. Justify a design using governance, integration, recovery and exit criteria rather than a preferred brand.
`)
add("CP1","Capstone 1 develops a defensible proposal around an actual problem, evidence and a feasible plan. These drafts use fictional organizations until the instructor authorizes external work. Follow the institution's real capstone and ethics requirements; this content creates neither permission to collect data nor a fabricated memorandum, endorsement or approval.",[research,privacy],`
Orientation, capstone purpose and scope|A capstone demonstrates an integrated solution and evidence of its usefulness. A research thesis and a development project can share inquiry methods but may emphasize different outputs. Start with a problem, affected users and observable outcomes. Institutional templates and agreements must come from the actual program, not a generated substitute.|A proposal says build an app for everyone. What scope information is missing?|It lacks a defined problem, target users, process boundary, expected outcome, constraints and evaluation plan. A technology choice alone is not a problem statement.|Read the approved course requirements and draft a learning contract plus a one-page fictional problem brief. Separate required institutional documents from documents that have actually been obtained.
Feasibility questions and evidence|Feasibility examines technical, operational, economic, schedule and ethical constraints before major implementation. A plausible feature is not necessarily maintainable or affordable. Use a question-and-evidence matrix: each important assumption should have a way to be checked and a decision it may change.|A clinic wants offline records synchronized later but no team member has tested conflict handling. Which feasibility issue needs a prototype?|Technical feasibility of offline synchronization needs a bounded prototype and explicit conflict cases. Operational training and privacy requirements also need investigation.|Prepare a feasibility matrix for a fictional offline booking tool with evidence sources, owners, uncertain assumptions and a go/revise/stop decision for each dimension.
Initial data gathering and instruments|A question should connect to a proposal decision. Interviews explore experience, observations record behavior and surveys summarize defined responses; none is automatically best. Neutral wording avoids steering participants. Plan consent, data minimization and storage before gathering information, following the real institutional review process.|Compare What delays occur during booking? with Do you agree our excellent app fixes booking? Which better investigates need?|The first is open and does not assume a solution. The second is leading and mixes approval of an app with evidence about the underlying process.|Create a five-question fictional interview guide and an observation checklist. Map every item to a feasibility decision and mark the approval needed before any real participant use.
Cost, schedule and alternatives|A feasibility comparison includes the current process, a minimal intervention and a proposed system. Estimate development, operation, training and maintenance separately. A schedule needs dependencies and contingency. Record uncertainty as a range or scenario rather than pretending an untested estimate is a measured fact.|A project has a 12-week limit; required access approval may take 4 weeks before a 10-week build can start. Is the serial plan feasible?|The stated serial path takes 14 weeks, exceeding the limit. Reduce scope, change dependencies legitimately or revise the schedule; silently omitting approval is not a valid fix.|Build a dependency schedule and fictional cost comparison for three alternatives. Identify the critical assumption and show a reduced-scope option that respects approvals.
Feasibility synthesis and proposal decision|A feasibility recommendation connects evidence to a bounded decision. Separate confirmed facts, estimates and unresolved risks. A proposal should identify success measures and conditions that trigger redesign. Negative findings are valuable because they prevent committing to an unsuitable solution; do not convert a stakeholder's interest into approval.|A prototype works on one device but the target users include several older devices. What can the recommendation claim?|It can claim the observed prototype result on that device, while broader compatibility remains unverified. Recommend representative testing before accepting the wider requirement.|Write a two-page fictional feasibility recommendation with a traceable evidence table, scope boundary, unresolved risks and explicit next approval steps. Retain dissenting or negative findings.
`)
add("CP2","Capstone 2 continues an approved project through ethical implementation and evaluation. Verify the actual proposal, permissions and institutional research-review documents before conducting external work. These drafts support review preparation and use fictional participant cases. They do not grant ethics clearance, assert an approval decision or generate genuine research results.",[research,privacy],`
Orientation and project-readiness review|A continuation project needs a baseline: approved scope, current implementation, unresolved risks and evaluation criteria. A capstone plan and a signed institutional approval are different artifacts. Keep a change log so new work can be traced to the authorized proposal rather than silently expanding participants or data use.|A team adds facial images to a project approved for anonymous timing observations. Can the original scope automatically cover the change?|No. The information and participant risk changed. The team must seek the applicable institutional review and authorization before collecting the added data.|Read the actual course and project documents. Produce a readiness matrix distinguishing verified approvals, missing documents, implementation status and proposed scope changes.
Ethics-review purpose and participant respect|Ethical review considers respect for people, benefits and harms, and fair participant selection. A usable consent process explains purpose, procedures, risks, choices and contacts in understandable language. Institutional review has its own scope and process; a generated consent form cannot itself approve a study.|A participant is told that declining a usability test will reduce a course grade. What ethical concern arises?|The threat undermines voluntary participation, especially where authority is unequal. Provide an appropriate alternative and seek guidance through the actual review process.|Analyze a fictional recruitment notice and revise coercive wording. Prepare questions for the institutional reviewer rather than claiming the revised notice is approved.
Protocol, information sheet and consent|A protocol specifies who participates, what happens, what information is recorded and how it is protected. A participant information sheet should match those procedures. Separate consent to participate from optional recording or publication choices. Maintain version consistency so participants do not agree to an obsolete procedure.|The protocol says no audio is retained but the consent form allows indefinite audio storage. What must happen before recruitment?|Resolve the contradiction through the actual review process and align the approved document versions. Neither conflicting text should be treated as permission to start.|Create a fictional document cross-check table for purpose, procedure, recording, retention and withdrawal. Highlight inconsistencies and draft questions requiring instructor/reviewer decisions.
Data handling, access and retention|Minimize identifiers and assign access by task. Pseudonymization replaces identifiers with codes but can remain reidentifiable; keep any linking key separately protected. A retention plan specifies purpose, duration, responsibility and deletion method. Backups and exported reports must be included in the plan.|A dataset replaces names with codes but stores the code-to-name key in the same shared folder. Is reidentification prevented?|No. The linked files allow identities to be reconstructed by anyone with folder access. Separate and restrict the key, minimize access and follow the approved handling plan.|Design a fictional data-flow and access matrix, including collection, coded analysis, backups, reporting and deletion. Mark where actual authorization is still required.
Review responses and authorized evaluation|Responding to review means addressing each concern with a traceable change or reasoned explanation. A pending or conditional response is not final approval. Evaluation must remain within the approved method, and deviations need the applicable reporting or amendment route. Do not invent participant outcomes to complete a report.|A reviewer asks for a shorter retention period; the team edits the protocol but not the participant sheet. Is the response complete?|No. All affected documents need consistent revision and the reviewer must receive the required response. The team still waits for the actual authorization status before proceeding.|Prepare a fictional point-by-point review response with document/version references, an evaluation-readiness checklist and a log of unresolved conditions; create no approval signatures or results.
`)
const math=ref('OpenStax: College Algebra','https://openstax.org/details/books/college-algebra-2e')
const stats=ref('OpenStax: Introductory Statistics','https://openstax.org/details/books/introductory-statistics-2e')
add("MATHPREP","Mathematical preparation connects symbols, graphs and quantities. Write the domain and units before manipulating an expression. Equivalent algebraic forms describe the same relationship only where their operations are defined. Use substitution and graphical reasoning to check a result; a calculator display without the assumptions is incomplete evidence.",[math],`
Algebra and equality|An equation asserts equality; solving seeks values that preserve it. Apply the same valid operation to both sides. Division by zero is undefined, and cancelling a factor requires checking whether it could be zero.|Solve 3x + 5 = 20 and verify by substitution.|x=5; substituting gives 15+5=20. Subtracting 5 then dividing by 3 preserves equality.|Solve three linear equations and annotate each operation. Include a zero-denominator expression and explain its excluded value.
Functions and domain|A function assigns exactly one output to each input in its domain. A formula may exclude inputs because of denominators or real square roots. A relation with two outputs for one input is not a function under that assignment.|Find the domain of f(x)=1/(x-2) over real inputs.|All real numbers except 2; at x=2 the denominator is zero.|Create input/output tables for a line and a reciprocal function. Mark excluded inputs and explain the vertical-line test on their graphs.
Linear and quadratic models|A linear model has constant rate of change. A quadratic includes a squared term and can model changing rates. Units and the realistic input interval matter; an algebraically valid value may lie outside a model's intended use.|Solve x squared minus 5x plus 6 equals zero.|Factor as (x-2)(x-3)=0, so the solutions are 2 and 3. Both satisfy the original equation.|Compare a linear cost model with a quadratic area model. Graph selected inputs and state which values make sense in each context.
Exponents and logarithms|Exponents describe repeated multiplication and extend under defined rules. A logarithm answers which exponent produces a positive value from a permitted positive base other than one. Check domain restrictions before simplifying or taking logarithms.|Solve 2 to the power x equals 32.|x=5 because five factors of 2 multiply to 32. The logarithmic statement log base 2 of 32 equals 5 is equivalent.|Build a doubling table, solve three exponential equations and check each by substitution. Contrast additive and multiplicative growth.
Angles and trigonometric ratios|For a right triangle, sine compares opposite to hypotenuse, cosine adjacent to hypotenuse and tangent opposite to adjacent. Identify the reference angle first. The Pythagorean theorem checks side consistency; degrees and radians must not be mixed silently.|A right triangle has legs 3 and 4 and hypotenuse 5. For the angle opposite 3, find sine and cosine.|Sine is 3/5 and cosine is 4/5. Their squared values sum to one.|Draw labelled triangles, compute three ratios and explain how swapping the reference acute angle changes opposite and adjacent sides.
`)
add("GE3","Mathematics in everyday decisions depends on clear patterns, logical claims and quantitative interpretation. Distinguish an attractive numerical pattern from a rule proven for every case. Each exercise asks for a representation, a result and an explanation that a non-specialist can inspect. Fictional money examples are arithmetic practice rather than personal financial advice.",[math,stats],`
Patterns and generalization|A finite sequence can fit many possible rules. A proposed pattern needs a stated generating rule and a check against the given terms. Prediction is conditional on that rule, not proof that nature or future observations must follow it.|Under the rule add successive odd numbers, extend 1,4,9,16.|The next difference is 9, giving 25. The rule yields square numbers, but the listed terms alone do not uniquely determine every future term.|Present two rules compatible with a short sequence. Explain why choosing a simple rule differs from proving it uniquely necessary.
Statements and logical implication|A statement has a truth value within a defined context. If P then Q does not imply if Q then P. One counterexample refutes a universal claim. Translate ordinary language carefully because only if and if reverse different relationships.|All squares are rectangles. Does being a rectangle imply being a square?|No. A rectangle with unequal adjacent side lengths is a counterexample to the converse.|Translate five daily claims into conditional form. Write converses and use diagrams or counterexamples to assess them.
Sets and counting|Sets model membership without repeated elements. The size of a union adds the two set sizes and subtracts their overlap. Counting people twice changes a total even when each separate group count is accurate.|Of 30 respondents, 18 use tool A, 15 use B and 8 use both. How many use either?|18+15-8=25 use at least one; 5 use neither, assuming the 30-person universe.|Draw a Venn diagram for a fictional survey, label every region and check that the region counts sum to the stated total.
Describing variation|A summary should communicate both typical value and variability. Mean and median answer different questions in skewed data. A percentage needs a denominator and period. Comparing two percentages without their populations can produce an unsupported conclusion.|Two groups report 80 percent success from 5 and 100 cases. Are their evidence amounts equal?|No. The first represents 4 of 5 and the second 80 of 100; equal percentages do not mean equal sample information.|Create two datasets with the same mean and different spread. Explain what a single average hides and draw a suitable comparison chart.
Rates and compound change|A rate relates quantities and needs units. Successive percentage changes multiply their factors; equal percentage rises and falls need not cancel. Keep the original and revised base explicit to avoid adding percentages from different denominators.|A fictional price of 100 rises 10 percent then falls 10 percent. Find the final amount.|It becomes 110 then 99 because the decrease uses 110 as its base; the net change is a 1 percent decrease.|Construct a spreadsheet comparing repeated percentage change with a fixed absolute change. Label bases, formulas and the limits of the model.
`)
add("IT105","Discrete mathematics studies finite or countable structures used in computing. Define the universe, assumptions and symbols before arguing. A worked case can illustrate a claim, while a proof must explain why it holds throughout the specified domain. Connect representations to program specifications rather than treating notation as an end in itself.",[ref('MIT OpenCourseWare: Mathematics for Computer Science','https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-042j-mathematics-for-computer-science-spring-2015/')],`
Propositions and truth tables|A proposition is true or false within its context. Conjunction requires both parts; disjunction permits either or both; negation reverses a truth value. A complete truth table checks every Boolean assignment rather than selected examples.|For P=true and Q=false, evaluate P and Q, P or Q and not Q.|The values are false, true and true respectively. Inclusive or does not require the two inputs to differ.|Build truth tables for three compound expressions and translate one program-access rule into propositional notation.
Implication and quantified claims|An implication fails when its premise is true and conclusion false. Universal claims cover every element; existential claims need at least one witness. Negating a universal introduces an existential counterexample, not a universal denial.|Negate every enrolled learner has a device.|At least one enrolled learner does not have a device. It does not mean that no learner has one.|Translate five system requirements using quantifiers. State the domain and construct a witness or counterexample for each selected claim.
Sets, functions and relations|A function assigns one output per domain element. Relations can describe broader associations. Injectivity prevents distinct inputs sharing an output; surjectivity covers the stated codomain. Both depend on the declared sets, not only the formula's appearance.|For f from {1,2,3} to {a,b}, with outputs a,b,b, is f injective or surjective?|It is not injective because 2 and 3 share b; it is surjective because both codomain elements occur.|Represent a small relation as ordered pairs and a table. Test function status, injectivity and surjectivity with explicit witnesses.
Counting and combinations|The multiplication principle counts successive independent choices under stated constraints. Permutations count order; combinations ignore it. Repeated elements and restrictions change the formula. Explain what one counted outcome represents before computing.|How many two-person committees can be chosen from five distinct people?|There are 5 choose 2 =10 committees; AB and BA represent the same committee.|Compare committees with ordered chair-and-secretary selections. Enumerate a small case and explain why the counting formulas differ.
Graphs and connectivity|A graph models vertices and edges; direction and weights add meaning. A path follows adjacent edges. A connected undirected graph has a path between every vertex pair. Real network properties require a model choice, so a graph omitting capacity cannot prove bandwidth sufficiency.|For edges AB, BC and CD, which single edge removals disconnect the four-vertex path?|Removing any of the three edges disconnects this path. Each is a bridge in the stated graph.|Draw a five-vertex network graph, list adjacency and find paths. Add a redundant edge and explain which single-edge failure it protects against.
`)
add("IT104","Research methods connect an answerable question to ethical evidence and a transparent analysis. A project should distinguish observation, interpretation and causal inference. Use fictional data and draft instruments until the actual institutional permissions are in place. A polished report does not compensate for a biased sample or an unsupported conclusion.",[research,stats,privacy],`
Questions and operational definitions|A research question identifies a population, phenomenon and relationship or outcome. Operational definitions explain what will be observed. Narrow questions improve feasibility and reduce ambiguity; a desired app feature is not automatically an empirical research question.|Rewrite students learn better with technology as a measurable descriptive question.|One option asks what completion times a defined class records for a specified practice task under a stated tool and procedure. This describes observations without asserting cause.|Draft three questions about a fictional learning process. Define variables, population, timeframe and evidence needed; separate descriptive and causal versions.
Literature and source evaluation|A literature review compares claims, methods and limitations rather than listing summaries. Prefer sources relevant to the actual question and record publication context. A citation verifies where a claim is attributed, not that the claim is necessarily correct or applicable.|Two articles report different results using different age groups and tasks. Must one be fraudulent?|No. Population, methods and context can explain differences. Compare their procedures and limitations before judging the disagreement.|Create an evidence matrix for three accessible readings with question, method, population, result and limitation. Quote sparingly and keep exact source locations.
Design and inference|Descriptive designs characterize observations; experimental designs manipulate conditions with appropriate controls. Random assignment differs from random sampling. Confounding can explain an apparent association. Choose a design that can support the intended claim and acknowledge what remains outside its scope.|Volunteers using a new app score higher than nonusers. Does the observation isolate an app effect?|No. Motivation, prior knowledge and selection may differ. A suitable comparison design and ethical controls are needed for stronger causal inference.|Compare two feasible designs for a fictional study. Identify sampling, assignment, confounders and the strongest justified conclusion for each.
Sampling and instruments|A sampling frame identifies who can be selected. Probability sampling needs a defined selection mechanism; convenience sampling limits generalization. Instruments require clear wording and consistent procedures. Pilot testing reveals ambiguity but is not proof of universal validity.|A survey asks how helpful and affordable a tool is in one rating. What is the measurement problem?|It is double-barrelled: helpfulness and affordability can differ. Separate them so respondents can answer each meaningfully.|Write a six-item fictional survey and a sampling plan. Revise leading or double-barrelled items and specify anonymous pilot feedback without collecting identities.
Ethics and an analysis plan|Ethical planning addresses consent, power differences, privacy and fair selection. Predefine how variables will be summarized, missing responses handled and results reported. A plan reduces opportunistic interpretation but must still permit disclosed corrections when assumptions fail.|A team deletes low ratings because they spoil the average. What should the analysis plan require?|Use predeclared quality criteria, retain valid negative responses and disclose exclusions with reasons. An inconvenient result is not a data-quality defect.|Prepare a protocol summary, consent draft and analysis table for fictional data. Mark required approvals and report missing values separately from zero.
`)
add("IT107","Human-computer interaction evaluates how people complete tasks through an interface. Start with user goals, context, access needs and constraints. Small prototypes let a team test assumptions before implementation. Observation needs voluntary participation and careful handling of information; classroom critique with fictional scenarios is sufficient for these initial drafts.",[wai,research],`
Users, tasks and context|Usability depends on effectiveness, efficiency and satisfaction for specified users and tasks. It is not simply visual attractiveness. Context includes device, environment, language and ability. A design assumption should be written so evidence could contradict it.|A beautiful form cannot be completed using a keyboard. Which user need was missed?|Keyboard access and task completion were missed. Visual polish does not establish effectiveness for users who cannot or do not use a pointer.|Describe a fictional student's course-registration goal, device and constraints. Write three testable usability assumptions and a task-success definition.
Requirements and ethical inquiry|Ask about actual goals and recent experiences rather than leading users toward a preferred feature. Separate an observed behavior from an interpretation. Personas should summarize evidence and limitations, not invent demographic stereotypes. Use fictional interview cases until permission exists for real inquiry.|Compare do you want our smart dashboard with describe how you checked your progress last time.|The second explores an existing task without assuming a dashboard is the solution. It may reveal different information needs.|Create an interview guide and evidence/interpretation table for a fictional learner journey. Map each proposed interface requirement to an observed or explicitly hypothetical need.
Feedback, mapping and error recovery|Controls need understandable labels, perceivable state and feedback after actions. Good mapping connects an action to its effect. Prevent avoidable errors and provide recovery for those that remain. A success message must correspond to an actual successful operation.|A Save button shows success before the server responds and then loses the record. What feedback contract failed?|The interface announced an unconfirmed result. It should show pending status, then confirmed success or a recoverable error while preserving entered work.|Sketch save, pending, success and retry states for a fictional form. Explain keyboard focus and how entered data survives a failure.
Accessible structure and alternatives|Semantic structure helps people navigate and understand an interface. Labels identify fields; headings organize content; alternative text communicates an image's purpose. Accessibility testing combines keyboard and assistive-technology checks with contextual review; an automated score alone is incomplete.|A form has a placeholder but no persistent label. What problem can arise after typing?|The field's identifying prompt disappears and may not provide a programmatic label. A persistent associated label supports continued understanding and navigation.|Create a paper or HTML form with labels, headings and explicit errors. Test its keyboard order and describe one issue an automated checker might miss.
Prototyping and usability evidence|A prototype should be detailed enough to test the current question. Task-based evaluation records completion, errors and observations without treating a tiny sample as a population estimate. Change one design hypothesis at a time when possible and document what evidence supports a revision.|Three fictional participants cannot find a course filter. Does this prove every student fails?|No. It reveals a concrete usability issue worth investigating and revising, but the sample does not justify a universal failure rate.|Build two low-fidelity filter alternatives and an ethical test plan. Define success, record fictional observations clearly and propose a revision tied to the observed difficulty.
`)
add("WMAD301","Accounting represents an organization's economic activity through consistent classifications and records. These exercises use fictional transactions and simplified assumptions, not tax or investment advice. Keep source transaction, journal entry and statement effect connected so a reader can verify both arithmetic and interpretation. Institutional accounting policies require the instructor's actual guidance.",[ref('IFRS: Conceptual Framework','https://www.ifrs.org/issued-standards/list-of-standards/conceptual-framework/')],`
Accounting equation|Assets represent resources, liabilities obligations and equity the residual interest under this introductory model. Each recorded transaction keeps assets equal to liabilities plus equity. Cash is one asset, not a synonym for revenue or profit.|An owner contributes 10,000 cash and the business borrows 4,000 cash. State the equation.|Assets are 14,000 cash, liabilities 4,000 and equity 10,000. Borrowing increases cash and obligations rather than revenue.|Create an equation table for five fictional startup transactions and verify equality after every row.
Accounts and debit-credit rules|Double-entry bookkeeping records equal debits and credits. Asset increases normally debit; liability and equity increases normally credit. Debit does not universally mean loss, and credit does not universally mean income. Classify the account before applying the direction.|A business buys equipment for 3,000 cash. Give the simplified entry.|Debit Equipment 3,000 and credit Cash 3,000. Total assets are unchanged at purchase because one asset replaces another.|Journalize five fictional cash/equipment transactions. Label account classes and show equal debit and credit totals.
Journals and ledgers|A journal records transactions in time order; a ledger groups changes by account. Posting preserves the link between entry and account balance. A balanced trial balance checks equality but cannot detect every omission or wrong-account entry.|Cash starts at 5,000, receives 2,000 and pays 800. What is its ending balance?|The cash balance is 6,200. The ledger should retain the references and dates behind each movement.|Post a small fictional journal to cash, equipment, payable and capital ledgers. Prepare a trial balance and describe an error that would still balance.
Accruals and period recognition|Accrual accounting distinguishes earning or incurring an item from receiving or paying cash. A receivable records an amount owed by a customer; a payable records an obligation. State the period and recognition assumptions before summarizing performance.|A service of 1,200 is earned in March and collected in April. Under the stated accrual assumption, when is revenue recorded?|Record March revenue and a receivable when earned; collection in April exchanges receivable for cash without recording the same revenue again.|Compare cash movement with earned revenue for four fictional timing cases. Explain why cash balance and period profit can differ.
Statements and reconciliation|An income statement summarizes period performance; a statement of financial position reports balances at a date. Revenue minus expenses is simplified profit, not necessarily cash generated. Reconcile statements to the underlying records and disclose omitted complexities.|Revenue is 8,000 and expenses 5,500. What is simplified profit, and does it prove cash rose by that amount?|Profit is 2,500. Receivables, payables, financing and asset purchases can make cash change different.|Prepare simplified statements from a fictional trial balance. Reconcile one cash-versus-profit difference and identify assumptions requiring instructor review.
`)
add("IT106","Programming 2 develops maintainable programs from explicit contracts, suitable structures and systematic checks. These drafts continue with Java as a concrete example; the instructor may adapt the language when approving the syllabus. Keep example data fictional and separate input validation, computation and presentation. Explain behavior through traces as well as execution.",[java],`
Methods and decomposition|A method should have one clear responsibility, named parameters and a documented return meaning. Splitting a program helps only when the boundaries reduce duplicated reasoning. Pure calculations can be checked independently of console input.|A total method accepts price 15 and quantity 4. What should it return, and which responsibility belongs elsewhere?|It returns 60 under nonnegative-input assumptions. Reading input and formatting a receipt belong outside the arithmetic method.|Refactor a fictional receipt calculator into input, computation and display methods. Test zero quantity and rejected negative values.
Arrays and iteration|An array stores a fixed number of indexed elements. Java indexes start at zero and valid positions end at length minus one. Iteration must handle an empty array and initialize accumulators consistently.|Find the sum and average of [4,6,8].|The sum is 18 and mean is 6. An empty array needs an explicit no-average result instead of dividing by zero.|Write methods for sum and maximum with documented empty-input behavior. Trace a three-element case and test boundaries.
Objects and encapsulation|An object combines state and behavior. Encapsulation protects invariants by exposing meaningful operations rather than unrestricted field changes. Constructors establish a valid starting state; setters still need validation and should exist only when change is intended.|A stock item must never have negative quantity. Why is a public writable quantity field problematic?|Any caller can violate the invariant. A validated operation such as receive or issue can enforce limits and explain failure.|Model a fictional stock item with validated operations. Show successful issue, insufficient stock and invalid input cases.
Exceptions and recoverable failure|An exception communicates a failure that normal return processing cannot complete. Catch failures where meaningful recovery is possible. Silently ignoring an error loses evidence; printing a stack trace to an end user can reveal irrelevant internals.|A file is absent. Should a program report that zero records were successfully loaded?|No. Missing input and a valid empty file are different outcomes. Report the failure clearly and preserve the opportunity to retry.|Build a small fictional record reader or its documented design. Distinguish missing file, malformed row and valid empty input.
Collections and tests|A list preserves sequence and duplicates, a set represents distinct membership and a map associates keys with values. Choose based on required operations. Tests need expected behavior, including duplicate keys and absent values, not only a happy-path demonstration.|Which structure maps a fictional student code to one contact record?|A map suits key-based lookup, with an explicit policy for duplicate keys. A list alone requires a search and does not enforce uniqueness.|Implement a fictional catalogue using a map and write checks for creation, lookup, duplicate handling and absent keys.
`)
add("IT203","Object-oriented programming models responsibilities and relationships while keeping invariants understandable. Use small Java examples with explicit contracts. A class hierarchy is useful when substitution preserves expected behavior, not simply because two nouns sound related. Tests should observe public behavior instead of depending on private implementation details.",[java],`
Objects and responsibilities|A class defines a type of object with state and operations. Allocate responsibility to the object that has the information needed to enforce the rule. A diagram should explain collaboration rather than merely list every noun in a problem.|A reservation must know whether its dates overlap another reservation. Which responsibility belongs with the reservation model?|Representing dates and testing overlap belong near reservation state; user-interface formatting belongs elsewhere. The design still needs a repository-level rule for simultaneous bookings.|Model a fictional booking with two operations and a date invariant. Draw its collaboration with a booking service.
Constructors and invariants|A constructor creates a usable object satisfying its invariant. Avoid partially initialized objects whose every caller must repair missing state. Encapsulation means controlling valid changes, not adding getters and setters for every field.|A positive-capacity room is constructed with capacity -2. What should the constructor do?|Reject the invalid input using a documented failure path rather than storing an impossible capacity. No valid object should escape with the broken invariant.|Design a Room class, valid examples and rejected cases. Explain which fields are immutable and why.
Composition and interfaces|Composition delegates to collaborating objects. An interface states behavior a caller may rely on while allowing different implementations. Depend on a narrow contract when variation is real; do not introduce layers without a concrete responsibility.|A receipt service needs to send a notification by email or an in-app channel. What shared contract can express that need?|A notifier operation can accept a message and report success or failure. Both implementations must honor that behavior without exposing channel-specific details to the caller.|Create a small notifier interface and two fictional implementations. Test the service using a recording implementation without sending real messages.
Inheritance and substitution|Inheritance permits reuse and specialization, but subclasses must preserve the promises callers rely on. Strengthening preconditions or changing meanings can break substitution. Prefer composition when the relationship is only code sharing rather than a valid behavioral subtype.|A read-only list subclass throws whenever an ordinary list caller adds an item. What contract concern arises?|If the base contract promised mutable addition, the subtype cannot safely substitute under that promise. A separate read-only interface expresses the restriction more clearly.|Analyze two proposed hierarchies and one composition alternative. Document supported operations and a counterexample to unsafe substitution.
Polymorphism and behavior checks|Polymorphic calls select implementation through a common contract. Tests should apply the same behavioral expectations to each implementation. Dispatch is not a reason to omit invalid-input or failure-path checks. Keep domain computation separate from external I/O for repeatable verification.|Two pricing strategies accept a price of 100; one returns 100 and one applies a stated 10 percent discount. What contract remains common?|Both return a nonnegative final price for valid inputs; the discount strategy returns 90 under the stated rule. Rounding and invalid values need a shared policy.|Implement two fictional pricing strategies and a reusable contract check covering normal, zero and invalid input behavior.
`)
add("IT205","Information management turns real-world concepts into consistent, useful records. Start with definitions and relationships, then express constraints in a relational design. These exercises use fictional people and a disposable database. A query result is trustworthy only when the underlying keys, missing-value meaning and access rules are understood.",[pg,privacy],`
Data, information and requirements|Data consists of represented observations; information interprets those observations for a purpose. Define entities, attributes and decisions before creating tables. A field dictionary should include meaning, allowed values and missingness rather than only a storage type.|A column contains 0 when no score is recorded. What ambiguity arises?|Zero could mean an earned zero or missing evidence. Use a distinct missing state so reports do not silently invent performance.|Write requirements and a data dictionary for a fictional course library. Identify three decisions each recorded field supports.
Entities, relationships and keys|A key uniquely identifies a row. Foreign keys represent links to existing referenced rows. Many-to-many relationships normally need a linking entity. A person's name is usually unsuitable as a durable unique key because names may repeat or change.|One student takes several courses and one course has several students. How should enrollment be modeled?|Use student and course entities plus an enrollment link containing their keys, with a uniqueness rule for each allowed pairing and period.|Draw an entity-relationship model for fictional students, courses and enrollments. State cardinalities and duplicate rules.
Normalization and dependencies|Normalization reduces update anomalies by separating facts according to their dependencies. A department name repeated in every enrollment can become inconsistent. Decomposition must preserve the ability to reconstruct meaningful relationships; splitting every field into its own table is not the goal.|A teacher email is copied into 100 course-membership rows and changes. What anomaly can occur?|Partial updates create conflicting emails for the same teacher. Store the teacher's contact fact in an appropriate entity and link by key.|Normalize a deliberately repeated fictional table through clear dependency steps. Show how a join reconstructs the needed view.
SQL selection and joins|SELECT chooses values, WHERE filters rows and joins combine related rows. An inner join omits unmatched records; a left join can retain them with nulls. Use parameters for untrusted values rather than string-built executable SQL.|Students A and B exist but only A has an enrollment. What does a student-left-enrollment join retain?|Both students remain; B has null enrollment fields. An inner join would omit B, which may or may not fit the report's purpose.|Write queries for enrolled learners and for learners without a course. Explain join choice and parameter handling using fictional records.
Quality, access and lifecycle|Useful records need accuracy, completeness, consistency and appropriate freshness. Access should follow purpose and least privilege. A retention plan distinguishes business deletion from preserving authorized audit evidence. Backups need a tested restore process, not just a success notification.|A report is accurate as of last month but labelled current. Which quality dimension is misrepresented?|Freshness is misrepresented. State the as-of time and refresh process instead of implying an up-to-date observation.|Create a quality/access matrix and a fictional correction workflow, including owner, validation, audit trail and retention decision.
`)
add("IT207","Integrative programming joins components across explicit data and failure contracts. Work with fictional local services or paper request/response examples. Correctness includes retries, invalid input, authorization and partial failure, not just a successful connection. Keep secrets outside source code and record what was actually executed versus designed.",[ref('IETF RFC 9110: HTTP semantics','https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110'),js],`
Integration boundaries|An interface contract defines inputs, outputs, errors and responsibilities. Integrating two working programs can still fail because they disagree on units, identifiers or timing. State the contract in examples that both sides can validate.|One service sends a date as day/month/year while another reads month/day/year. Why can valid-looking data be wrong?|Ambiguous representation gives different meanings to the same characters. A documented unambiguous format and validation are needed.|Specify a fictional booking interface with identifiers, timestamps, status values and three valid/invalid examples.
HTTP request and response|HTTP methods express request semantics and status codes communicate the result. A transport success does not prove the business operation completed correctly. Separate authentication, authorization, validation and unexpected failure in the response design.|A request receives 200 but its body says no booking was saved. What should a client avoid?|It must not announce a saved booking solely from the transport status; validate the agreed business response contract and handle failure consistently.|Draft local request/response examples for create, read and denied access. Explain method, status and body meaning.
Serialization and schema checks|Serialization converts structured values to a transferable form. JSON does not preserve every language-specific type automatically. Validate required fields, bounds and allowed values at the receiving boundary and reject incompatible versions clearly.|A JSON amount arrives as the string "ten" where a finite number is required. What should happen?|Reject it as invalid input with a useful field error rather than silently converting it to zero.|Create a bounded fictional JSON schema and a table of accepted and rejected payloads, including missing, extra and wrong-type fields.
Retries and idempotency|A timeout leaves uncertainty about whether a remote operation completed. Retrying a non-idempotent action can duplicate an effect. A stable operation identifier and stored result can make retries safe under a defined contract.|A payment-like simulation times out after recording its operation. What must a repeated request preserve?|It must reuse the same operation identifier and return the existing result without recording a second effect. No real payment is required.|Design a local duplicate-request simulation with operation IDs. Show first success, lost response and safe retry states.
Testing partial failure|Integration tests need controlled dependencies and observable boundaries. Test unavailable services, malformed replies, expired authorization and delayed responses. A rollback or compensating action must match the business effect; hiding an error does not undo it.|A booking succeeds but its notification fails. Should the system silently delete the booking?|Not automatically. The contract may retain the booking and retry notification, while showing the true status. Define recovery from the actual business requirement.|Create a failure matrix and local checks for a fictional booking-plus-notification flow. Record expected final state and retry ownership.
`)
add("IT208","System integration and architecture describes how responsibilities collaborate under constraints. Use diagrams together with concrete scenarios, data ownership and quality requirements. A design must explain normal behavior and failure, including organizational responsibility. These drafts propose fictional systems and do not authorize changes to institutional services.",[cloud,cyber],`
Requirements and quality attributes|Functional requirements describe behavior; quality attributes describe measurable properties such as latency, availability and maintainability. A vague word like fast needs a workload, threshold and measurement method. Conflicting requirements need a visible tradeoff.|Rewrite the system must be fast as a testable fictional requirement.|For example, 95 percent of catalogue reads finish within a stated threshold under a specified load and dataset. The chosen threshold needs stakeholder approval.|Write five functional and five measurable quality requirements for a fictional library, identifying assumptions and acceptance evidence.
Context and component boundaries|A context diagram shows users and external systems; a component view shows internal responsibilities. Boundaries should clarify ownership and interfaces. A diagram with arrows but no data or direction cannot explain an integration contract.|A diagram connects portal and identity service with an unlabeled line. What information is missing?|Request direction, exchanged data, authentication, failure behavior and ownership are unclear. Label the actual interaction and trust boundary.|Draw context and component views for a fictional portal and annotate three interfaces with payload and failure contracts.
Architecture alternatives|Layered, service-based and event-driven designs offer different coordination and operational costs. Choose from the workload and team rather than a trend. More services add deployment, observability and failure-handling responsibilities.|A two-person team builds one small local inventory tool. Does splitting every feature into a service guarantee maintainability?|No. Separate deployment and coordination can increase complexity. A modular single application may meet the requirements more simply.|Compare two architecture options against the same fictional requirements. Document benefits, costs and the evidence that would change the choice.
Data ownership and consistency|A shared fact needs an authoritative owner and a policy for propagation. Immediate consistency and delayed updates affect user expectations. Events can be duplicated or delayed, so consumers need identifiers and reconciliation rather than assuming one perfect delivery.|An order event arrives twice at a stock service. What should the integration contract require?|Recognize the same operation and avoid decrementing stock twice; retain enough evidence to reconcile state. Delivery alone does not guarantee exactly-once business effects.|Specify ownership and duplicate handling for a fictional order/stock flow, including a delayed-event recovery scenario.
Deployment and operational readiness|Deployment needs configuration, compatible versions, monitoring, rollback and responsible people. A successful build does not prove production readiness. Define a health signal tied to actual user behavior and preserve evidence when a release fails.|A service process is running but cannot reach its database. Is process uptime a complete health check?|No. Check a safe representative dependency path and report degraded readiness. Avoid a health probe that creates business records.|Create a deployment checklist and failure drill for a fictional portal, including staged verification, rollback criteria and data-compatibility concerns.
`)
add("IT209","Web systems connect semantic documents, styles, scripts and server contracts. Start with readable structure and progressive behavior before adding complexity. Use original assets and fictional form data. Validate browser input for usability and validate again on the server for trust; a hidden button or field is not an authorization boundary.",[wai,js,ref('MDN: Learn web development','https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Learn_web_development')],`
Web request lifecycle|A browser requests resources over a network and interprets HTML, CSS and scripts. URLs identify resources; HTTP communicates requests and replies. A page can fail because of DNS, transport, server or rendering issues, so record the observed boundary.|HTML loads but a stylesheet request returns 404. What behavior is likely and what does the status mean?|Content may render without the intended styles. 404 indicates the requested stylesheet resource was not found under that response contract.|Draw a page-load sequence and inspect a fictional or local page's resource requests without collecting private traffic.
Semantic HTML|HTML elements express structure and meaning. Headings form a hierarchy, links navigate and buttons perform actions. Use associated labels for inputs. Choosing an element by appearance alone can remove keyboard and assistive-technology behavior.|A clickable div has no keyboard handler. Which native element better expresses a form action?|A button provides action semantics and native keyboard interaction. Styling can preserve the intended appearance without losing meaning.|Build a local course-information page with headings, links, a labelled form and a meaningful table caption.
CSS and responsive layout|CSS controls presentation through cascade, inheritance and layout rules. Relative sizing and flexible grids help content fit different widths. Overflow should be measured rather than hidden blindly, because clipping can make controls unreachable.|A table fits its desktop container. Should a permanent horizontal scrollbar be required?|No. Auto overflow permits scrolling only when content genuinely exceeds the available width; preserve access on narrow screens.|Style a local course page at phone and desktop widths. Check focus visibility, readable wrapping and genuine overflow rather than only a screenshot size.
JavaScript and the DOM|The DOM represents the document as nodes. Event handlers should update state and communicate feedback without losing focus or meaning. Treat external text as data; inserting it as executable markup can create injection risks.|A user-entered name contains angle brackets. How should a plain greeting display it?|Insert it as text through a safe text API rather than interpreting it as HTML. Validation and authorization still belong at their own boundaries.|Add a local form interaction with explicit pending, success and error states. Test keyboard use and a name containing markup-like characters.
Forms and server trust|Client validation provides quick feedback but can be bypassed. The server must validate shape, bounds and authorization independently. A useful error identifies the field and recovery action while preserving user work. Sensitive credentials should not enter browser source or logs.|A browser disables a delete button for other owners. Does that prevent a crafted unauthorized request?|No. The server must independently check the authenticated actor and record ownership before mutation.|Design a fictional form API contract with valid, invalid and unauthorized requests. Explain where each validation and permission check belongs.
`)
add("IT2061","Information assurance protects confidentiality, integrity and availability in a defined system. Work only with fictional cases or an explicitly authorized local lab. Threat analysis should lead to proportionate controls and verification. The purpose is defensive reasoning, including recovery and accountability, rather than collecting real credentials or probing third-party systems.",[cyber,privacy],`
Assets and security objectives|An asset has value to people or an organization. Confidentiality limits disclosure, integrity protects correctness and availability supports timely use. The same incident can affect several objectives. Prioritize based on the actual consequences, not a universal threat ranking.|A public file exposes student contacts while a corrupted copy changes scores. Which objectives are affected?|The exposure affects confidentiality; the altered scores affect integrity. Either incident may also disrupt service and trust.|Inventory five fictional portal assets, their owners and consequences of disclosure, alteration and loss of availability.
Threats, vulnerabilities and risk|A threat can exploit a vulnerability; risk considers likelihood and consequences under stated assumptions. A control reduces some risk and may introduce operational cost. A qualitative risk matrix supports discussion but is not a measured probability unless data supports it.|A shared administrator password remains unchanged after staff departure. Identify a weakness and a control.|Shared credentials prevent reliable individual accountability and leave continued access risk. Individual accounts, revocation and appropriate multifactor controls reduce that exposure.|Create a fictional threat register with asset, weakness, consequence, control owner and evidence needed to verify improvement.
Identity and access control|Authentication establishes an identity claim; authorization decides permitted actions. Least privilege limits access to what a task needs. Server and database boundaries must enforce ownership even when the interface hides controls. Review permissions when duties change.|A signed-in learner guesses another learner's record ID. What must the server verify?|It must verify authority for that specific record, not merely that someone is signed in. Deny unrelated record access.|Write an access matrix for fictional learner, instructor and administrator roles. Include denied reads and writes with an audit requirement.
Cryptographic purposes|Encryption protects confidentiality with a key; a cryptographic hash supports integrity checking; a digital signature supports origin and integrity under its key assumptions. They solve different problems. Password storage needs a purpose-built salted password-hashing scheme rather than reversible plaintext recovery.|A file and its hash are both replaced by an attacker. Does matching the new hash prove an authentic original?|No. Integrity evidence must come from a trusted reference or authenticated channel. A hash alone does not establish who supplied it.|Build a paper data-protection map identifying where encryption, trusted checksums, signatures and password hashing serve distinct needs.
Response and recovery|Incident response needs detection, containment, preservation, recovery and learning under authorized procedures. Backups require restore checks and access protection. Do not destroy possible evidence or announce an unverified scope. A response record distinguishes confirmed facts from hypotheses.|A backup job reports success but no restore has been tried. What recovery evidence is still missing?|Successful restoration of the required records within the intended recovery window remains unverified. Backup creation and recovery are different checks.|Draft a fictional incident timeline and recovery exercise with responsibilities, evidence handling, communication approvals and restore acceptance criteria.
`)
add("IT2101","Advanced database work reasons from correctness and workload before optimizing. Use a disposable PostgreSQL database with fictional records. Keep constraints and access checks in place while measuring alternatives. A faster query is valuable only if it returns the correct authorized result and its maintenance, storage and write costs remain acceptable.",[pg],`
Transactions and invariants|A transaction groups changes that should succeed or fail together. Database constraints enforce durable invariants even when different clients write concurrently. Application checks alone can race. Define what must remain true before choosing a transaction boundary.|Two requests both observe one remaining seat. Can two separate read-then-insert checks safely reserve it?|Not without appropriate database coordination and constraints. Both can pass the initial read and oversubscribe the seat.|Design a fictional seat-reservation transaction with a capacity invariant, concurrent-request scenario and clear failure outcome.
Indexes and query plans|An index can reduce search work but costs storage and write maintenance. Selectivity and access pattern determine usefulness. Inspect the plan and actual workload; the mere presence of an index does not prove a query uses it or becomes faster.|A table is queried frequently by course_id and date. What evidence should guide a proposed index?|Inspect filters, ordering, row counts and the actual plan, then compare representative reads and write overhead. Column order depends on the queries.|Create a fictional query workload, propose one index and document the plan observations needed before claiming a performance improvement.
Joins and aggregation correctness|Joins can multiply rows when relationships are one-to-many. Aggregating after an unintended multiplication can overcount. Identify the intended grain of a report and aggregate each fact at the correct level before combining results.|One course has two materials and three attempts. Joining both collections directly produces how many combinations?|Six combinations can occur, so summing attempts or materials naively duplicates facts. Separate aggregation or a correctly designed relation avoids the error.|Construct a tiny fictional dataset showing join multiplication and write a corrected per-course report with reconciled totals.
Concurrency and isolation|Concurrent transactions can observe changes differently depending on isolation and locking. A consistent snapshot does not automatically protect every cross-row invariant. Keep transactions short and lock in a consistent order when necessary; retries must not duplicate business effects.|Two transfers lock account rows in opposite orders. What failure can arise?|A deadlock can occur when each waits for a row held by the other. Consistent lock ordering and a safe retry policy reduce the problem.|Trace two fictional transfers, identify the deadlock cycle and redesign the lock order and idempotent retry contract.
Recovery and authorized reporting|Recovery planning includes backup, restore, migration compatibility and data access. Reporting accounts should receive only required rows and fields. Measure recovery from a real disposable restore rather than assuming a backup file is usable. Retain business audit requirements during corrections.|A reporting user needs course totals but is granted all private student fields. Which principle is violated?|Least privilege and data minimization are violated. Provide only the required authorized aggregate view or scoped query.|Prepare a fictional reporting-access matrix and disposable restore plan, including row-count reconciliation and a measured-versus-planned evidence log.
`)
add("IT211","Social and professional computing issues require technical understanding and public accountability. Analyze stakeholders, rights, consequences and uncertainty together. Use fictional professional cases and cite the applicable readings. A legal concept is not an automatic legal conclusion for a real dispute, and professional responsibility can demand more than minimal technical compliance.",[privacy,ip,cyber],`
Professional responsibility|Professional work affects users, coworkers and the public. Competence includes recognizing limits, documenting evidence and escalating risks. A deadline does not justify claiming tests were run when they were not. Separate personal opinion from an applicable professional obligation.|A developer discovers an untested data-loss path before release. What should a responsible report contain?|Describe the reproducible or suspected path, impact, uncertainty, proposed check and responsible decision owner. Concealing it removes informed choice.|Write a fictional release-risk memo with evidence, affected stakeholders, options and a clear statement of unverified assumptions.
Privacy and purpose|Personal information should have a defined purpose and appropriate access, retention and safeguards. Technical ability to collect more data is not a reason to do so. Consent, contractual duties and other legal bases require the actual context and institutional review.|A study app requests contacts even though its feature uses no contact data. What should be questioned?|Necessity, purpose, disclosure and retention should be questioned. Remove unrelated collection rather than treating broad permission as a design goal.|Audit a fictional app's fields against its stated purpose and produce a minimized data-flow with access and retention responsibilities.
Intellectual property and licenses|Code, media, inventions, brands and confidential information have different protection and licensing questions. Open source still has license conditions. Keep provenance, permitted use and obligations with each dependency or asset; a public repository is not automatically public domain.|A team copies a library without checking its license because it is free to download. What is missing?|The permission terms and obligations for copying, modifying and distributing are missing. Availability and reuse rights are separate facts.|Create a fictional dependency/asset ledger, identify unresolved rights and describe how the release changes if permission cannot be established.
Fairness and digital inclusion|Systems can exclude people through device requirements, language, disability, connectivity or biased data. Fairness requires a defined decision context and evidence about affected groups. A population-wide average can hide systematic access barriers.|A course portal works only with constant high-speed access. Which learners may be disadvantaged?|Learners with intermittent connectivity or limited data may be excluded even when the interface works on a developer's device. Investigate accessible low-bandwidth alternatives.|Map a fictional learner journey under three access constraints and propose testable improvements, explaining tradeoffs and remaining limitations.
Accountability and ethical disagreement|Reasoned disagreement identifies principles, evidence, affected people and decision authority. Whistleblowing, disclosure and incident handling require careful channels and factual accuracy. Keep a record of the concern and response without exposing unrelated personal information.|A manager asks for a dashboard with invented engagement figures. What should a professional alternative do?|Display actual evidence and explicit missing states, explain the consequence of fabrication and propose legitimate measurement. Attractive charts do not justify false records.|Write a fictional response memo with an ethical analysis, an evidence-preserving alternative and an authorized escalation route.
`)
add("WMAD302","Mobile systems operate under changing connectivity, device capability, permission and lifecycle conditions. A design must preserve useful state without collecting unnecessary information or wasting resources. These starter weeks use platform-neutral models and fictional data; the instructor selects the actual supported development stack before approving the syllabus.",[ref('Android developer fundamentals','https://developer.android.com/guide/components/fundamentals'),wai],`
Mobile platforms and constraints|A mobile platform supplies lifecycle, input, storage and networking APIs. Battery, screen size and intermittent connectivity are design constraints, not unusual exceptions. Distinguish the application from the operating-system services it requests.|A note disappears when the operating system recreates a screen. What requirement was missed?|Relevant state must survive permitted lifecycle transitions using an appropriate persistence mechanism; keeping it only in a temporary view is insufficient.|Map a fictional note-taking journey across launch, backgrounding and restoration, marking temporary and durable state.
Interaction and accessible layouts|Touch targets, readable text and keyboard or assistive navigation affect task completion. Layout should adapt to available space rather than assume one fixed device. Labels must communicate purpose independently of decorative icons.|An icon-only delete control has no accessible name. What does a nonvisual user lack?|The action's purpose is unavailable to assistive technology. Supply a meaningful accessible name and suitable confirmation/recovery behavior.|Prototype a mobile course list at two widths and document touch, focus, labels and text-scaling checks.
Lifecycle and state ownership|A screen can pause, disappear or be recreated. State ownership determines which data can be reconstructed and which must be saved. Restoration must not replay a purchase, submission or paid inference merely because a view returns.|A reload repeats an assessment submission command. Is that valid restoration?|No. Restore the authorized view and read current state; mutations require their own explicit intent and idempotency contract.|Draw a lifecycle state diagram for a fictional quiz reader, separating navigation references from unsaved answers and commands.
Local data and synchronization|Offline storage needs a defined source of truth and conflict policy. A queued update can be retried or arrive after another change. Use stable identifiers and version checks; last-write-wins is a policy with potential data loss, not a universal solution.|Two devices edit the same note offline. What must happen when they reconnect?|Detect the version conflict and apply the chosen reconciliation policy, preserving evidence rather than silently assuming both edits are compatible.|Design a fictional offline-note synchronization table covering new records, duplicate retries, edits and conflicts.
Permissions and network failures|Request only permissions needed for a feature and explain their purpose. A denied permission is an expected state. Network timeouts need clear feedback and safe retry without exposing tokens or losing user work.|A course reader asks for contacts access. Is that justified by reading a document?|Not by the stated feature. Avoid unrelated permission collection and keep document access scoped to authorization.|Create a permission/failure matrix for a fictional mobile reader, including denial, expired session, timeout and successful recovery.
`)
add("WMAD305","Systems administration maintains reliable services through controlled configuration, least privilege and recoverable changes. Work in a disposable local environment or a paper simulation. Record commands, versions and observed results without exposing credentials. Never apply a classroom change to institutional hosts merely because a command is available.",[ref('Ubuntu Server documentation','https://documentation.ubuntu.com/server/'),cyber],`
Inventory and baselines|An inventory records devices, operating systems, services, owners and support status. A baseline distinguishes intended configuration from an unexplained change. Record the observation time; stale inventory cannot prove present readiness.|A server list has no owner or update date. What operational decisions become unreliable?|Maintenance responsibility and freshness are unknown, so the list cannot reliably establish who must act or whether the configuration still exists.|Create a fictional five-host inventory with ownership, service purpose, versions, dependencies and review dates.
Accounts and least privilege|Separate individual identities from shared service roles. Grant only necessary permissions and review them when duties change. Administrative work needs traceability; a shared unrestricted password undermines accountability.|A temporary helper receives permanent administrator access. Which lifecycle action is missing?|Access should be bounded to the task and time, then reviewed or revoked. Temporary need does not justify indefinite privilege.|Design an account lifecycle matrix covering request, approval, provisioning, review and departure for a fictional lab.
Files, permissions and configuration|File permissions and service identities control who can read or change configuration. Keep secrets outside public code and logs. Validate a change before replacing working configuration and retain a known-good recovery path.|A configuration file includes a secret and is readable by every local user. What should be reviewed?|Restrict necessary access, move or protect the secret appropriately and rotate exposed credentials through the authorized process. Hiding the filename is insufficient.|Prepare a fictional directory/permission plan and a reversible configuration-change checklist in a disposable environment.
Services, logs and monitoring|A running process does not prove successful user operations. Monitor relevant dependencies and user-visible behavior. Logs need timestamps, context and protection against sensitive-data leakage. Alert thresholds should identify actionable conditions with a responsible owner.|A web process runs but every database query fails. What should readiness report?|Readiness should reflect the failed dependency and affected service path, not only process existence. Keep probes safe and bounded.|Create a service dependency map and fictional log examples for normal, degraded and failed states, with a response owner.
Backups, patching and recovery|Backups require restoration checks and protected retention. Patches need compatibility review, staged verification and rollback planning. Recovery objectives state acceptable data loss and outage duration; they must be tested rather than assumed.|A daily backup is the only recovery copy. Can it guarantee no data loss after any failure?|No. Changes since the last usable backup may be lost, and restore time remains unmeasured. State actual recovery limits.|Design a fictional patch-and-restore exercise with acceptance checks, rollback triggers and an honest executed-versus-planned evidence log.
`)
add("WMAD307","Mobile game development connects rules, feedback, input and resource limits. Begin with a small playable loop rather than a large asset catalogue. Use original or licensed assets and fictional player data. A prototype's enjoyment claim requires observed play evidence; working graphics alone do not establish a coherent or accessible game.",[ref('Godot engine documentation','https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/'),wai],`
Game loops and rules|A game loop updates state and renders feedback repeatedly. Rules specify permitted actions, success and failure. Separate game state from visual effects so the result can be traced and tested without relying on animation timing.|A collect-and-avoid game has no loss condition or finish rule. What is missing from its specification?|The player cannot determine completion or failure. Define the goal, permitted actions, obstacle effect and end-state conditions.|Write a one-page fictional mobile-game brief and trace its input/update/render loop through a complete round.
Scenes, objects and coordinates|A scene organizes objects and responsibilities. Coordinates describe position relative to a reference frame; screen and world coordinates can differ. Naming units and origin prevents movement errors and inconsistent layouts.|An object moves from x=10 to x=25 in the same coordinate system. What displacement occurred?|The horizontal displacement is +15 units. Its physical screen distance still depends on scale and coordinate mapping.|Create a paper or local scene with player, obstacle and interface objects. Label coordinates, ownership and event responsibilities.
Input, movement and elapsed time|Input should express an intended action independently of device sampling rate. Movement based on elapsed time can reduce frame-rate dependence. Touch input also needs cancellation, accidental-input handling and an accessible alternative where feasible.|Speed is 60 units per second and elapsed time is 0.5 seconds. Find displacement.|The displacement is 30 units under constant speed. Moving a fixed amount per frame would vary with frame rate.|Implement or trace elapsed-time movement at two frame intervals and compare the final position, documenting numerical assumptions.
Collisions and game state|Collision detection identifies overlap or contact; game rules decide the consequence. A single contact may persist across frames, so repeated events need a state or timing policy. Separate score changes from decorative feedback.|A collectible adds one point on every overlapping frame. What prevents one object awarding many points?|Mark it collected and remove or disable its reward before processing subsequent overlap events. The state change must occur once.|Design a collectible state machine and test normal contact, persistent overlap and a repeated event with explicit expected scores.
Feedback, testing and mobile performance|Feedback should show what happened and what the player can do next. Measure performance on the intended class of device, including input latency and resource use. Playtesting records observations; invented engagement scores cannot prove fun.|A game is smooth on a desktop but stutters on a phone. What claim remains unverified?|Mobile performance remains unverified. Profile the target device and simplify the measured bottleneck rather than assuming one cause.|Prepare a small playtest and performance plan with task success, frame observations, accessibility concerns and privacy-preserving notes.
`)
add("WMAD308","Internet of Things systems connect sensing, computation, communication and action in a physical context. Begin with a simulated sensor and a safe read-only use case. Sensor values have units, uncertainty and timing; connectivity does not guarantee correct measurement. These drafts require no mains wiring, remote device probing or unattended physical control.",[ref('NIST: Internet of Things','https://www.nist.gov/internet-things-iot'),cyber],`
IoT system boundaries|An IoT system includes device, sensor, communication, processing and user or actuator. A system diagram must show where data originates and what action it supports. An Internet connection alone does not make a measurement trustworthy.|A dashboard displays a temperature with no unit or observation time. What information is missing?|The value cannot be reliably interpreted without unit, time, sensor identity and relevant quality context. A current screen does not guarantee a current reading.|Draw a simulated room-monitoring system with labelled data and trust boundaries, using fictional values only.
Sensors, units and calibration|A sensor converts a physical condition into a signal under a specified range and uncertainty. Calibration compares readings with a reference; resolution is not the same as accuracy. Preserve raw and converted values so errors can be investigated.|A sensor reads 23 when a reference reads 21 in one trial. Does one offset prove a universal correction?|No. It suggests a difference at that condition; examine multiple points, repeatability and reference quality before adopting a correction model.|Build a fictional calibration table across three reference values and explain offset, variation and the limitations of the simulated data.
Sampling and local processing|Sampling interval affects which changes can be observed. Local filtering can reduce noise but may hide short events. Missing readings should remain missing rather than becoming zero. State time basis, sequence and invalid-value handling.|A missing temperature message is stored as zero. What false interpretation can result?|The system may report a real freezing event instead of communication failure. Store an explicit missing/invalid state and a timestamp.|Create a simulated ten-reading series with missing and noisy values. Compare raw values with a stated simple filter and retain anomalies visibly.
Messaging and delivery|Messages need device identity, time, units and a schema. Network delivery may be delayed or duplicated. Topic or endpoint naming does not replace authorization. Define how the receiver detects stale readings and repeated sequence numbers.|The same sensor message is received twice. Should a cumulative event counter necessarily increment twice?|No. If it is the same identified event, deduplicate under the contract. Distinct events and repeated delivery are different cases.|Design a fictional sensor payload and receiver table for normal, delayed, duplicate and malformed messages in a local simulation.
Security and maintenance|Connected devices need bounded access, update responsibility and a retirement plan. Default shared credentials and unmanaged firmware increase exposure. Fail-safe behavior should be explicit when connectivity or data quality is lost; a dashboard cannot silently imply normal operation.|A simulated alarm receives no reading for an hour. What should its status communicate?|Report stale or unavailable evidence and the defined safe response rather than displaying the last value as current. Escalation depends on the actual use case.|Create a fictional IoT lifecycle checklist covering identity, updates, stale data, access, recovery and retirement, without controlling physical equipment.
`)
add("WMAD309","Application development combines requirements, interfaces, data, verification and maintenance. Emerging technology is an option to evaluate, not a reason to ignore a simpler established solution. Use fictional cases and a bounded local prototype. Record uncertainty, costs and user impact before claiming that a technology improves a working process.",[wai,cyber,ref('NIST AI Risk Management Framework','https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework')],`
Problems and technology hypotheses|A problem brief states who needs what outcome and why the current process falls short. A technology hypothesis predicts an improvement that can be tested. Separate user value from novelty and identify an alternative requiring less infrastructure.|A team proposes AI because the app must be modern. What evidence is missing?|The user problem, baseline, expected benefit, suitable data and evaluation method are missing. A deterministic workflow may meet the need more clearly.|Write a fictional problem brief and compare an established implementation with an emerging-technology hypothesis using the same outcome measures.
Architecture and vertical slices|A vertical slice implements a small end-to-end user outcome across interface, logic and storage. It reveals integration assumptions earlier than isolated screens. Keep trust boundaries and failure states visible even in a prototype.|Three attractive screens exist but no record can be saved and read back. Is the user journey complete?|No. The end-to-end persistence and authorization path remains unverified. A minimal working slice should include a confirmed round trip.|Specify a fictional create-and-read journey with validation, permission, persistence and error acceptance checks.
Data contracts and lifecycle|A data contract defines meaning, format, ownership and change compatibility. New technology may need extra information, but collection must remain purpose-bound. Plan migration and deletion alongside creation. Missing fields and unknown values need explicit semantics.|A new model feature requires a field absent from old records. What should the migration plan address?|Define defaults or explicit unknown states, compatibility and backfill evidence; do not invent historical observations to fill the field.|Design a fictional versioned schema and migration checklist with old/new readers, unknown values and rollback considerations.
Evaluation and uncertainty|Evaluate an emerging component against a relevant baseline and representative cases. Accuracy alone may hide subgroup errors, latency, cost and failure consequences. Keep human review where the use case needs it and disclose unverified coverage.|A classifier succeeds on five hand-picked examples. What broad claim is unjustified?|The examples do not establish reliable performance on the target population. Define a suitable evaluation set, metrics and error review before generalizing.|Prepare a fictional comparison plan with baseline, test cases, false-positive/false-negative costs and a human review path, without inventing results.
Delivery and maintainability|A release needs reproducible configuration, dependency records, monitoring and an exit plan for external services. A prototype success does not establish long-term reliability. Document the smallest useful feature and the conditions for adopting or rejecting the emerging component.|A provider disappears and the application cannot export its data. Which design risk materialized?|Vendor dependency and missing portability prevent continuity. An export contract and fallback path should be evaluated before adoption.|Write a fictional release-readiness memo with costs, observability, data export, rollback and a decision rule for keeping or removing the new technology.
`)
add("WMADE2","This elective draft proposes a web/mobile quality-engineering track because no approved specialization outline is recorded for this offering. The instructor must confirm or replace the track before approval. The first weeks teach requirements, testing, accessibility, security and delivery through fictional local examples; they do not claim an existing approved elective title.",[wai,cyber],`
Elective scope and quality goals|Quality means meeting explicit user and operational needs. Agree on the elective's actual specialization before selecting tools. Define a defect by an unmet requirement, not personal taste. A quality plan connects a risk to evidence.|A team says a page is good because it looks complete. What is still missing?|Task, accessibility, correctness and failure acceptance criteria remain unspecified. Visual completeness alone cannot establish quality.|Draft a proposed elective learning contract and a fictional course-reader quality plan for instructor confirmation.
Testable requirements|An acceptance example gives inputs, action and expected result. Include permission and boundary cases as well as normal use. Tests that merely repeat implementation details can pass while user behavior is wrong.|An age field allows a stated range 18 to 60 inclusive. Which boundaries deserve checks?|Test 17,18,60,61 and invalid/missing input. Expected behavior follows the inclusive contract, not an arbitrary interface implementation.|Write acceptance examples for a fictional enrollment form, including duplicates, permission denial and recoverable network failure.
Accessible interaction verification|Keyboard access, semantic labels and perceivable feedback support task completion. Use manual interaction alongside automated checks. A zero-warning report does not prove that instructions, reading order or error recovery make sense.|A dialog opens but keyboard focus remains behind it. Which behavior fails?|The active interaction context and focus management fail. Users may operate hidden content instead of the dialog.|Create a local dialog or paper interaction model and document focus entry, tab order, Escape behavior and focus return checks.
Security and data-boundary checks|A server must validate the actor, record and action independently of visible controls. Keep fictional data and test only authorized local services. Logging should support diagnosis without exposing passwords, tokens or unrelated personal information.|A user changes a record ID in a request. Which check must still run?|Record-level authorization must run for the requested ID. A successful sign-in alone does not grant access to every record.|Build a fictional access-test matrix for owner, unrelated user and anonymous requests, with expected denials and audit evidence.
Release evidence and maintenance|A release record should state what was tested, where and with which limitations. Keep a rollback path and meaningful health observations. Pending checks must remain pending rather than becoming fabricated passes.|A test was designed but never executed. How should the release report describe it?|Label it planned/unexecuted and state the remaining uncertainty. A written test case is not evidence of a passing run.|Prepare a fictional release dossier with actual local observations or clearly unexecuted plans, known issues and rollback criteria.
`)
add("IT401","IT seminars connect current professional learning to critical evaluation and communication. A seminar attendance record differs from understanding or verified implementation. Choose topics with the instructor and evaluate claims using dated primary documentation. These drafts create preparation and reflection tasks rather than inventing speakers, events, attendance certificates or industry outcomes.",[cyber,ref('NIST AI Risk Management Framework','https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework')],`
Learning goals and seminar selection|A useful seminar addresses a specific knowledge gap and credible learning objective. Record title, organizer, date, format and source without treating promotion as independent evidence. A planned event remains planned until actual participation.|A poster promises guaranteed expertise after one hour. What should a learner evaluate?|Check scope, speaker evidence, prerequisites and realistic outcomes. Attendance cannot guarantee competence or replace practice.|Draft a seminar-selection rubric and three learning questions for a proposed topic, clearly marking any event not yet verified.
Primary sources and dated claims|Technical claims need product/version context and primary documentation. A dated vendor example may be accurate for its environment but unsuitable elsewhere. Separate a demonstrated feature from marketing and speculation.|A slide gives an old API call without a version. What must be checked before reuse?|Verify the applicable official version, current signature and migration notes. Do not assume the copied call still works.|Build a claim/source/version matrix from a real accessible technical reading, with exact links and remaining uncertainties.
Professional questions and discussion|A strong question connects a claim to assumptions, evidence or consequences. Respectful discussion can challenge reasoning without attacking a speaker. Distinguish clarification, evaluation and application questions so discussion supports learning.|Which is more useful: is this the best tool or under which workload did the comparison hold?|The workload question identifies conditions that determine applicability; best has no defined criterion or context.|Prepare five seminar questions across clarification, evidence, risk, access and application, explaining the learning purpose of each.
Demonstration and reproducibility|A demonstration is persuasive when its inputs, environment and outputs can be inspected. Record what was actually observed and avoid generalizing from a single success. Reproduce only within an authorized local environment and protect secrets.|A live demo succeeds once. Does it prove production reliability?|No. It establishes one observed execution under specific conditions. Broader reliability requires suitable repeated and failure-case evidence.|Plan a bounded local reproduction of one seminar concept and document environment, expected result, actual observation or unexecuted status.
Reflection and knowledge transfer|A reflection connects new learning to prior understanding, evidence and future action. A useful transfer artifact explains context and limits for another learner. Do not invent attendance, certificates or professional endorsements.|A reflection lists slide titles but no changed understanding. What is missing?|It lacks synthesis, evaluated evidence, limitations and a justified next action. A topic list alone does not show learning.|Create a one-page technical brief and personal learning plan with source links, one unresolved question and a small follow-up exercise.
`)
add("OJT","Practicum learning requires the actual institution and host organization's agreements, supervision and policies. These pre-placement and early-placement drafts prepare responsible work habits and evidence recording. They create no employer assignment, signed agreement, attendance hours, completion certificate or permission to access workplace systems. Use fictional workplace cases until the real placement is authorized.",[privacy,cyber],`
Placement readiness and responsibilities|A placement needs verified host, supervisor, duties, schedule and institutional requirements. Clarify escalation and acceptable access before starting. A generated checklist helps preparation but cannot stand in for an actual signed document or authorized assignment.|A learner receives an informal request to inspect a company database before placement approval. What should be clarified?|Verify supervisor authority, permitted duties, access and institutional approval before acting. Interest in the task is not authorization.|Prepare a placement-readiness checklist with verified, pending and not-applicable states, leaving real missing approvals explicitly pending.
Professional communication|Work updates should state task, evidence, blocker and next action. Report uncertainty early enough for a supervisor to respond. Keep confidential details out of public portfolios and personal messaging channels.|An update says done although a test is blocked by missing access. What is a clearer report?|State the completed part, the unverified test, required access and the next authorized step. Do not claim a blocked result passed.|Write three fictional supervisor updates: normal progress, a blocker and a discovered defect, using concise evidence-based language.
Work planning and safe changes|Break work into reviewable tasks with acceptance criteria, dependencies and rollback. Confirm the environment before a change; production and training systems have different consequences. A task assignment does not imply unlimited administrative rights.|A trainee tests a cleanup command on live records. Which preparation was missed?|Environment confirmation, permitted scope, backup/recovery and supervisor review were missing. A disposable test should precede authorized production action.|Create a fictional change plan with acceptance checks, supervisor review, test environment and rollback responsibilities.
Confidentiality and ethical practice|Access workplace information only for assigned purposes and follow the actual confidentiality policy. Screenshots, logs and portfolios can expose personal or commercial data. Use anonymized or synthetic demonstrations only when permitted and keep credentials outside reports.|A portfolio screenshot includes customer names and an access token. What should happen before sharing?|Do not publish it. Remove confidential information and obtain the required permission; exposed credentials need authorized incident handling and rotation.|Audit a fictional work-log excerpt for sensitive information and produce a safe learning summary without claiming employer approval.
Learning evidence and reflection|A work log records actual dates, tasks, supervision and evidence. Reflection explains skills developed and remaining gaps. Proposed hours are not completed hours, and invented supervisor feedback cannot establish performance.|A learner plans 20 hours next week. How should those hours appear in a completion report?|They remain scheduled or planned, separate from verified completed hours. Record actual work only after it occurs under the required supervision.|Design a practicum evidence log and weekly reflection template with planned/actual distinctions, supervisor verification fields and no fabricated entries.
`)
const valuesContext='Christian Values Education connects moral reflection with responsible action and respectful dialogue. Examine motives, effects, duties and the dignity of people affected by a decision. Personal faith disclosures are optional; fictional cases permit thoughtful participation without revealing private beliefs. The instructor confirms institutional theological framing and required readings before approving these starter lessons.'
add('CVE1A',valuesContext,[],`
Values and moral decisions|A value expresses what a person considers important; a moral decision must also consider other people's dignity and interests. A preference alone does not justify harm. Explain a choice using reasons that another person can examine.|A teammate values speed and copies another person's work without credit. What competing consideration is ignored?|Honesty and respect for the creator and group are ignored. Efficiency cannot alone justify misrepresentation.|Analyze a fictional group-work dilemma using values, affected people, options and a reasoned choice.
Dignity and respect|Human dignity treats people as worthy of respect rather than instruments for a goal. Respectful disagreement addresses ideas and actions without humiliating the person. Listen for perspectives excluded by the loudest voice.|A group dismisses a quiet member before hearing an idea. What should change?|Provide a fair opportunity to contribute and assess the idea on reasons. Participation should not depend only on confidence or status.|Write a respectful dialogue showing disagreement, clarification and inclusion without assigning real classmates private motives.
Conscience and informed judgment|Conscience involves judging a proposed action, but responsible judgment needs information, reflection and willingness to correct error. Sincere belief does not make every consequence harmless. Ask what facts would alter the decision.|A learner repeats a rumor believing it helps a friend. What should be checked first?|Verify the claim, consider affected people and avoid spreading unconfirmed personal information. Good intention does not establish truth.|Create a decision journal for a fictional rumor case with known facts, uncertainties, duties and a correction path.
Empathy and service|Empathy seeks to understand another perspective without assuming identical experiences. Service should respond to expressed needs and preserve agency. Helping is not a license to make choices for someone without listening.|Volunteers choose a project without asking the community. What is missing?|Community participation and evidence of an actual priority are missing. A useful intervention needs listening and shared decision making.|Design a fictional listening plan with open questions, consent and a way for community members to reject or revise the proposal.
Integrity and accountability|Integrity aligns stated commitments with actual conduct. Accountability includes admitting error, correcting effects and preventing repetition. A private mistake still matters when it affects others; a public apology alone does not repair the consequence.|A learner discovers an incorrect contribution after the group report is sent. What action supports integrity?|Inform the group, correct the record through the appropriate channel and explain the impact honestly instead of concealing it.|Write a fictional correction plan with disclosure, repair, responsibility and a future verification habit.
`)
add('CVE2A',valuesContext,[],`
Community and belonging|A community combines relationships, shared practices and responsibilities. Belonging should not require excluding people with different experiences. Evaluate a rule by its purpose and effects rather than by familiarity alone.|A club announces meetings only through a paid app. What access issue arises?|Members without that app or data may be excluded. An accessible communication alternative supports fair participation.|Map a fictional community's members, shared goals and barriers, proposing one inclusion improvement.
Solidarity and shared responsibility|Solidarity recognizes interdependence and responsibility beyond immediate personal advantage. It differs from treating people as passive recipients. Sustainable help builds participation and respects local decision making.|A donor insists on a project the recipients do not need. Is the gift automatically beneficial?|No. The recipients' priorities and ability to decline matter. Resources do not replace respectful collaboration.|Compare two fictional service proposals using expressed need, participation, continuity and accountability.
Respect across beliefs|Dialogue across beliefs seeks understanding without requiring agreement. Describe another view accurately before evaluating it. Private belief is not a valid basis for humiliating someone or excluding them from a shared academic task.|A team assigns tasks based on assumed religious identity. What fairer basis exists?|Use relevant skills, consent, availability and task requirements rather than stereotypes about belief.|Write a dialogue protocol for a fictional diverse group, including clarification, disagreement and protection of private disclosures.
Care and stewardship|Stewardship links present use of resources to responsibility for others and future users. Evaluate consumption, maintenance and waste together. A symbolic environmental gesture should not substitute for evidence of its practical effect.|A group buys disposable decorations to advertise resource conservation. What tension should be examined?|The message and material practice may conflict. Compare reuse, necessary function and waste rather than assuming the slogan proves stewardship.|Prepare a fictional event resource plan with reuse, maintenance, waste and accessibility decisions.
Reconciliation and repair|Reconciliation requires truth, responsibility and voluntary participation; it does not require ignoring harm. A repair process should protect those affected and clarify future boundaries. Forgiveness cannot be demanded as a substitute for accountability.|A team pressures a harmed member to move on without correcting a false accusation. What is missing?|The false record needs correction and the affected person needs a voice and appropriate protection. Forced closure is not repair.|Design a fictional conflict-repair conversation with facts, acknowledgment, practical correction and agreed boundaries.
`)
add('CVE3A',valuesContext,[],`
Vocation and purposeful work|Vocation can be examined as the use of abilities in responsible service. Career goals include livelihood, growth and effects on others. A prestigious title does not by itself establish meaningful or ethical work.|A developer must choose between impressive features and a needed accessibility fix. What purpose should guide reflection?|Consider users' actual needs and the consequences of exclusion alongside project obligations. Visibility alone is not a sufficient moral criterion.|Write a fictional career-purpose statement connecting abilities, community needs and professional responsibilities.
Justice and fair opportunity|Justice examines fair treatment, burdens and access to opportunity. Equal treatment can still disadvantage people facing unequal barriers. State which fairness principle a proposal uses and consider its effect on affected groups.|A course requires constant online attendance despite documented access barriers. Which concern arises?|Formal equality of one rule may conceal unequal ability to comply. Examine reasonable alternatives while preserving learning outcomes.|Compare two fictional participation policies using fairness, learning evidence and practical access.
Ethical technology and responsibility|Technology embeds choices about information, incentives and participation. Moral responsibility extends beyond whether code executes. Identify who benefits, who bears risk and who can challenge a harmful result.|An app ranks students using unverified activity counts. What ethical problem follows?|The ranking can misrepresent learners and affect opportunity without trustworthy evidence. Missing records must not be invented or treated as established behavior.|Analyze a fictional education app with a stakeholder/risk table and an evidence-based redesign.
Conflict and principled dialogue|A principled dialogue separates people from the disputed action and asks for reasons, evidence and shared duties. Compromise is useful only where it does not conceal a serious harm or erase a legitimate boundary.|A team votes to hide a privacy defect to meet a deadline. Does majority agreement settle the concern?|No. The rights and risks of affected users remain relevant even when most teammates prefer release.|Write a fictional disagreement memo proposing an authorized review, evidence needed and a responsible interim action.
Service reflection and humility|Reflection connects action, actual effects and changed understanding. Humility recognizes incomplete knowledge and invites correction. Do not infer a community's gratitude, consent or benefit from a volunteer's intention alone.|A project report claims everyone benefited without asking participants. What evidence gap remains?|The claim lacks participant perspectives and a defined benefit measure. Report observed outputs separately from unverified outcomes.|Create a fictional service reflection with outputs, evidence of effects, uncertainty and participant-led next steps.
`)
add('CVE4A',valuesContext,[],`
Leadership as responsibility|Leadership includes stewardship of authority, information and opportunity. A leader should make reasons inspectable and invite appropriate challenge. Position alone does not establish moral correctness or competence in every domain.|A leader assigns desirable tasks only to friends. Which responsibility is neglected?|Fairness and accountability in using authority are neglected. Task decisions need transparent relevant criteria.|Design a fictional team charter with task criteria, participation and a safe route for raising concerns.
Accountability and transparent evidence|Accountability requires truthful records and a process for review. A successful-looking metric can conceal missing or manipulated observations. Leaders should preserve uncertainty rather than demand a convenient number.|A dashboard has no recorded learner activity. Should a leader invent scores to show progress?|No. Show the missing evidence and arrange legitimate measurement and support. Fabrication undermines decisions and trust.|Write a fictional management note explaining missing data, responsible next steps and measures that can be collected honestly.
Organizational culture and moral courage|Culture is reinforced by incentives and everyday responses to mistakes. Moral courage can involve raising a concern respectfully despite pressure. Use appropriate channels and factual evidence rather than exposing unrelated private information.|A team praises speed but punishes anyone who reports defects. What behavior might this encourage?|People may conceal problems to avoid punishment, increasing hidden risk. Reward truthful reporting and responsible correction.|Analyze a fictional workplace incentive and propose a reporting/correction practice with safeguards against retaliation.
Common good and sustainable choices|The common good considers conditions enabling people to participate and flourish together. A decision should examine distribution of benefits and burdens over time. Immediate savings may transfer costs to less powerful people or future users.|A cheap platform cannot export records or support disabled users. Which longer-term concerns matter?|Portability, inclusion, maintenance and dependency may outweigh initial savings. Evaluate lifecycle consequences rather than price alone.|Prepare a fictional procurement reflection with affected groups, lifecycle effects and a justified recommendation.
Commitment and reflective practice|A durable commitment names an action, context, accountability and review point. Reflection should be revisable when new evidence appears. Avoid claiming moral perfection; acknowledge limits and practical support needed to keep the commitment.|A pledge says always be ethical with no review process. How can it become actionable?|Name concrete conduct, foreseeable pressures, a trusted accountability route and a time to review outcomes.|Create a personal or fictional professional ethics plan with three observable commitments, support needs and a correction process.
`)
add("GE1","Purposive communication chooses language, structure and medium for a defined audience and goal. Meaning depends on context as well as grammar. These activities use fictional academic and workplace situations. Make claims traceable, protect private information and revise using specific reader feedback rather than equating a polished tone with accuracy.",[ref('Purdue OWL: General writing','https://owl.purdue.edu/owl/general_writing/index.html')],`
Audience, purpose and context|A message changes with the reader's knowledge, needs and authority. State the action or understanding sought before drafting. A formal tone is not a substitute for clear purpose, and unnecessary jargon can obscure an otherwise simple request.|An instructor receives urgent please help with no course or issue. What context should be added?|Identify the course, concrete problem, relevant timing, attempted steps and requested action while keeping the message concise.|Write versions of one fictional technical problem for a peer and an instructor, explaining audience-based changes.
Organization and coherence|A coherent message develops one main idea through logically connected support. Topic sentences and transitions should reveal the relation between ideas. Repetition without new evidence lengthens a text without strengthening its argument.|A paragraph begins with course access and abruptly discusses cafeteria prices. What revision is needed?|Separate unrelated ideas or explain a genuine connection. Each paragraph should advance a clear purpose.|Outline and revise a fictional three-paragraph request, marking main point, evidence and expected response.
Claims, evidence and attribution|A claim needs appropriate evidence and a clear distinction between observation and inference. Paraphrase changes expression while preserving meaning and attribution. Citation does not authorize copying an entire source or eliminate the need to evaluate credibility.|A report says all students prefer an app after two informal comments. What should change?|Limit the claim to the observed comments and disclose the evidence boundary. Broader preference needs suitable inquiry.|Create a claim/evidence table for a fictional campus proposal, replacing unsupported generalizations with qualified statements.
Professional correspondence|A useful email or memo names the issue, relevant facts, requested decision and timing. Subject lines help readers prioritize. Keep tone respectful and direct, with attachments identified and private information minimized.|A memo requests approval but never says which option is recommended. What is missing?|The reader lacks a concrete decision and rationale. State the recommendation, alternatives and evidence needed to act.|Draft a fictional request memo and reply addressing one unresolved question, using exact dates and clear responsibility.
Presentations and multimodal meaning|An oral presentation needs a clear route through the argument, readable visuals and accessible alternatives. Slides support the speaker rather than reproduce an entire script. Practice timing and invite questions that test the evidence.|A slide uses an unlabeled chart to prove improvement. What must the audience know?|They need measure, units, population, period, source and comparison context before interpreting improvement.|Prepare a three-minute fictional proposal with a labelled chart, speaking outline, text alternative and a peer-feedback revision.
`)
add("GE2","Understanding the self uses reflective and social inquiry to examine identity, learning and relationships. These lessons are educational, not diagnosis or treatment. Personal disclosure is optional: fictional cases can meet the objectives. Distinguish a useful concept from a claim that one theory explains every person's experience.",[],`
Identity and context|Identity includes roles, relationships, experiences and interpretations that may change across contexts. A label describes one aspect rather than the whole person. Reflection should leave room for complexity and avoid inferring private traits from appearance.|A quiet learner is labelled unmotivated. What alternative explanations deserve consideration?|Language, comfort, context, fatigue or preferred participation style could affect behavior. One observation does not establish motivation.|Create a fictional identity map separating roles, observable behavior, interpretations and unanswered questions.
Self-concept and social comparison|Self-concept concerns beliefs about oneself; social comparison can inform or distort those beliefs. A selected public image is not a complete record of another person's life. Use specific evidence rather than global labels such as failure.|A learner compares one low result with a peer's best public result. Why is the comparison limited?|The evidence is selectively sampled and may differ in task, preparation and context. It cannot define either person's overall worth or ability.|Analyze a fictional comparison and rewrite it as a specific learning question with a fair evidence basis.
Learning, goals and feedback|A learning goal names a skill, practice and review point. Feedback is useful when tied to observable work and a possible next step. Improvement requires evidence across attempts rather than self-criticism alone.|A goal says become better at coding. How can it be made actionable?|Specify a bounded skill, such as tracing three loop examples, a practice schedule and a check of explanation accuracy.|Build a fictional weekly learning plan with one skill goal, practice evidence, feedback route and revision criterion.
Emotions and interpersonal understanding|Emotions provide information about experience but do not automatically prove another person's intention. Pause to distinguish feeling, observation and interpretation. Empathy asks questions and respects boundaries rather than claiming to know exactly how someone feels.|A delayed reply is interpreted as deliberate rejection. What remains unknown?|Availability, connectivity, workload and intent remain unknown. Seek clarification without treating the interpretation as an established fact.|Write a fictional dialogue that names an observation, expresses a concern respectfully and invites clarification without demanding private disclosure.
Digital identity and boundaries|Online identity is shaped by posts, platforms and audiences. Privacy choices require attention to persistence, sharing and context. A private reflection need not become public evidence of learning; use appropriate boundaries and safe fictional examples.|A class asks everyone to post personal struggles publicly. What alternative supports learning?|Offer private or fictional reflection meeting the objective without compulsory public disclosure of sensitive experiences.|Create a fictional digital-boundary plan covering audience, consent, permanence and respectful group communication.
`)
add("GEELECES","Environmental science studies interactions among living systems, physical conditions and human decisions. Use units, boundaries and evidence when evaluating sustainability claims. A local intervention can shift impacts elsewhere, so compare material and energy flows across a stated lifecycle. These starter exercises use fictional inventories, not invented field measurements.",[ref('UNEP: Plastic pollution','https://www.unep.org/en/what-we-do/chemicals-and-pollution-action/plastic-pollution')],`
Systems and ecological relationships|An ecosystem includes organisms and their physical environment. Energy flow and material cycling describe different processes. Define a system boundary so outside inputs and waste do not disappear from the analysis.|A campus calls itself zero waste after sending rubbish elsewhere. What boundary problem arises?|Waste has moved beyond the chosen boundary rather than ceased to exist. The destination and downstream handling still matter.|Draw a fictional campus material-flow map with inputs, reuse, waste destinations and missing evidence.
Food webs and interdependence|Food webs represent feeding relationships; changes can affect connected populations. A model simplifies reality and needs a stated scope. Correlation in two population trends alone does not identify the ecological cause.|Two species decline together after a disturbance. Does that prove one caused the other's decline?|No. Shared habitat change, resource loss or other factors may affect both. Examine mechanisms and additional evidence.|Create a small fictional food web and trace two possible effects of a disturbance, marking assumptions rather than predictions as facts.
Energy and resource accounting|Energy use needs a rate, duration and unit. Power multiplied by time gives energy under a constant-power assumption. Compare equivalent services and lifecycle boundaries rather than only the operating draw of one device.|A 50-watt device runs 4 hours. Find its energy use.|It uses 200 watt-hours, or 0.2 kilowatt-hours, under the stated constant-power assumption.|Build a fictional device energy inventory and compare an efficiency option, showing hours, units and excluded manufacturing effects.
Waste and circular design|Waste prevention, repair and reuse can reduce demand for new material, but practical suitability depends on collection, durability and safe handling. Recycling is one pathway, not proof of a closed loop. E-waste needs authorized handling.|A device is replaced yearly for a minor feature. Which alternatives should be evaluated?|Consider maintenance, repair, software support and continued use before replacement, including whether the existing device still meets requirements.|Prepare a fictional equipment lifecycle plan with repair, reuse, secure data removal and authorized end-of-life handling.
Environmental claims and evidence|A sustainability claim needs a baseline, measure, boundary and comparison period. Relative reductions can hide growing absolute use. Document uncertainty and unintended consequences rather than relying on green branding.|Energy per request falls 20 percent while requests double. What happens to total modeled energy?|The new total is 0.8 times 2, or 1.6 times the original, a 60 percent increase under equal boundaries.|Audit a fictional green-IT claim using both per-unit and total measures, with an evidence plan for what remains unknown.
`)
add('GE4',"Historical reading examines how accounts were produced, preserved and interpreted. A primary source offers evidence from a relevant context but can still be partial, mistaken or interested. Separate the source's claim, your interpretation and external corroboration. Use attributable documents and exact locations; do not invent quotations, eyewitnesses or archival discoveries.",[ref('National Historical Commission of the Philippines','https://nhcp.gov.ph/')],`
Sources and historical questions|A historical question defines a time, place and problem. Primary and secondary describe a source's relation to the inquiry, not an automatic ranking of truth. A later analysis may illuminate an earlier account's limits.|Can a memoir written long after an event be used as primary evidence?|Yes for relevant testimony or memory, but its timing, audience and reliability need examination. It is not automatically a complete eyewitness record.|Choose an accessible Philippine historical document and record creator, date, audience, purpose, location and an answerable question.
External and internal criticism|External criticism examines authenticity and provenance; internal criticism examines meaning, perspective and plausibility. A genuine document can contain biased claims. Keep uncertainty visible when author, date or custody is unknown.|An unsigned scan circulates with no original archive link. What should be checked?|Locate provenance, document context and a reliable copy before attributing it. Visual age alone does not authenticate it.|Build a source-criticism worksheet for a real accessible document, distinguishing confirmed metadata from unknown fields.
Perspective and corroboration|Accounts differ because of position, purpose, access and later memory. Corroboration compares independent evidence rather than counting copies of the same story. Disagreement can reveal context rather than simply require choosing a favored narrator.|Three websites repeat one unattributed claim. Is that three independent confirmations?|No. Repetition may derive from one source. Trace the attribution chain and seek independent evidence.|Compare two accounts of one Philippine historical topic, identifying agreement, disagreement, dependence and unanswered questions.
Philippine nationhood and contested narratives|Nationhood can be studied through institutions, movements, communities and competing claims. Avoid treating one elite account as every person's experience. Define whose perspective a document represents and which groups are absent.|A political declaration claims a whole population speaks with one voice. What question should a historian ask?|Ask who authored and endorsed it, who could participate and whose views were excluded. The declaration itself does not prove universal agreement.|Analyze an accessible Philippine political document with a claim/evidence table and a note on missing perspectives.
Evidence-based historical argument|A historical argument links a claim to selected evidence while addressing alternatives and limitations. Citation should identify the actual source and location. A conclusion may remain provisional; uncertainty is preferable to invented certainty.|A paper quotes a sentence without its surrounding qualification. What interpretive risk arises?|The quotation may reverse or exaggerate the source's meaning. Read surrounding context and represent the limitation accurately.|Write a short historical argument using two accessible sources, exact references, a counterinterpretation and an explicit evidence limitation.
`)
add('GEELECPPC',"Popular culture examines widely circulated practices, media and meanings in Philippine contexts. Audiences interpret and adapt culture rather than merely consume it. Analyze a specific artifact with its production, platform and reception context. Use original descriptions and permitted excerpts, avoiding stereotypes or invented claims about what all Filipinos believe.",[ip],`
Culture, popularity and context|Popularity can mean reach, repeated participation or visibility, and those measures differ. Cultural meaning depends on context and audience. A trending platform metric is not a complete measure of social importance.|A video has many views. Does that prove all viewers endorse its message?|No. Viewing can reflect curiosity, criticism or repeated playback. Endorsement requires different evidence.|Select a public Philippine cultural artifact and define the question, audience and evidence without copying the whole work.
Representation and identity|Representation selects features and viewpoints rather than neutrally reproducing every experience. Ask who speaks, who is depicted and who is absent. A fictional character cannot establish a fact about an entire social group.|A comedy portrays one regional accent as unintelligent. What should analysis consider?|Examine the stereotype, power relations, narrative context and audience interpretations instead of treating the portrayal as evidence about actual speakers.|Create a representation analysis of a permitted short example, separating description, interpretation and evidence of reception.
Media production and platforms|Production decisions, distribution systems and revenue incentives influence what becomes visible. Recommendation systems can amplify content without proving its truth or cultural value. Distinguish creator intent from observed circulation.|A platform recommends a clip repeatedly. Does recurrence establish factual accuracy?|No. Recommendation reflects a distribution process, not an independent verification of the claim.|Map the production, distribution, monetization and audience feedback of a fictional cultural campaign.
Appropriation, adaptation and rights|Cultural adaptation can create new meanings while raising attribution, consent, commercial and power concerns. Legal permission and community respect are related but distinct questions. Do not assume a public cultural practice is unrestricted material for any commercial use.|A brand copies a community design without consultation. Is credit alone enough to settle the issue?|No. Rights, community authority, context and consent need examination; attribution does not automatically resolve them.|Prepare a fictional cultural-use proposal with provenance, affected community, permissions, benefit-sharing questions and an option not to proceed.
Critical participation and synthesis|Critical participation evaluates evidence and one's own role in circulation. A share can amplify a misleading or harmful claim. An analysis should explain its scope and acknowledge alternate audience readings.|A satire is reshared as literal news. What context should a responsible commentary restore?|Identify its genre, original context and evidence, while avoiding further amplification of unsupported claims as facts.|Write a short review of a Philippine popular-culture artifact with source links, alternative readings and a responsible sharing decision.
`)
add('CORDI101',"Cordillera study recognizes diverse histories, languages, communities and knowledge systems. Do not collapse the region into one uniform identity. Community members and authorized cultural knowledge holders should guide interpretation and permission. Use public, attributable sources for these drafts; do not solicit sacred, restricted or private knowledge to complete an academic exercise.",[ref('Indigenous Peoples Rights Act, RA 8371','https://lawphil.net/statutes/repacts/ra1997/ra_8371_1997.html'),ref('National Commission for Culture and the Arts','https://ncca.gov.ph/')],`
Region, community and diversity|A geographic region can contain many distinct communities and histories. Administrative boundaries, language and cultural identity do not always coincide. Identify the specific community and source rather than applying one example to everyone.|A presentation says all Cordillera communities share one tradition. What is the problem?|It erases diversity and requires evidence beyond one example. Name the particular community, context and source and avoid universal claims.|Prepare a public-source regional map with labelled source dates and a note distinguishing administrative and cultural descriptions.
Oral history and responsible sources|Oral accounts are situated evidence with a teller, audience and purpose. Documentation needs permission, attribution preferences and awareness of restricted knowledge. A published account does not authorize demanding more private detail from a community member.|A learner wants to record an elder without discussing use or storage. What is missing?|Informed permission, purpose, audience, retention and the right to decline or limit use are missing.|Design an unexecuted oral-history protocol with consent and community consultation; use a public account for analysis instead of conducting an unauthorized interview.
Land, livelihoods and social relations|Land and livelihood can carry economic, social, historical and cultural meaning. Study the specific relationship through credible evidence rather than romanticizing it. Legal categories and lived experience need careful distinction.|A report treats ancestral land only as vacant commercial space. Which perspectives are omitted?|Community history, rights, livelihoods and cultural relationships may be omitted. Examine applicable evidence and community perspectives.|Create a fictional stakeholder map for a land-use question with explicit rights, evidence gaps and consultation requirements.
Heritage, ownership and respectful use|Heritage preservation includes context, authority and continuity, not only collecting objects or images. Some knowledge or practices have access restrictions. A classroom assignment does not override community decision making.|A cultural image is online. Does availability grant unrestricted commercial reuse?|No. Examine provenance, rights, community protocols and the intended use. Public visibility is not universal permission.|Prepare a fictional heritage exhibit plan using permitted public materials, with source attribution, access limits and community review questions.
Contemporary change and community agency|Communities actively interpret change through education, migration, technology and local decisions. Avoid framing people as frozen in the past. A balanced account includes benefits, tensions and the community's own choices.|A tourism report calls a community authentic only if it avoids modern tools. What assumption should be challenged?|It denies community agency and treats culture as static. Continuity and change can coexist under locally determined meanings.|Write a public-source reflection on one contemporary Cordillera issue, distinguishing documented community voices, your interpretation and unresolved questions.
`)
add('GE5',"Science, technology and society studies how knowledge, institutions and values shape each other. Scientific evidence has methods and limits; technology choices distribute benefits, costs and power. Use a defined case and dated sources rather than assuming innovation always means progress. Classroom scenarios are fictional and do not establish medical or environmental outcomes.",[ref('UNESCO: Ethics of science and technology','https://www.unesco.org/en/ethics-science-technology'),cyber],`
Knowledge, evidence and society|Scientific inquiry connects questions, methods and revisable explanations. Social institutions influence funding, participation and application. A scientific claim needs evidence suited to the question rather than only a confident speaker.|A product testimonial claims universal benefit. What makes it insufficient scientific evidence?|Anecdotes lack an appropriate comparison, sampling and method for a universal claim. Investigate study design and limitations.|Create a claim/method/evidence table for a public technology claim, marking what remains unverified.
Technology as a social choice|Design choices embed priorities such as speed, cost, privacy and accessibility. Different stakeholders can value outcomes differently. A technical possibility is not an automatic reason to implement it.|A city installs a digital-only service. What social design question arises?|Consider who lacks devices, access or required skills and how alternatives preserve participation. Efficiency for one group can shift burdens to another.|Map benefits and burdens of a fictional digital service and propose an accessible alternative with measurable goals.
Risk and uncertainty|Risk analysis states the hazard, exposure, likelihood assumptions and consequences. Uncertainty can concern missing data or model limitations. Precaution and evidence gathering require proportionate decisions rather than pretending every risk is known.|A new tool has no documented failure cases. Does that prove it has no risk?|No. Absence of recorded evidence may reflect limited testing or reporting. Define plausible failure paths and needed evaluation.|Build a fictional risk/uncertainty register with evidence needs and reversible pilot conditions.
Innovation and environmental effects|Innovation can reduce one impact while increasing another. Evaluate lifecycle boundaries, total use and rebound effects. A per-device efficiency claim does not establish lower overall consumption.|A more efficient device is replaced twice as often. What should the comparison include?|Include manufacturing, operation, repair, disposal and service delivered over the same period. Efficiency alone is an incomplete lifecycle comparison.|Create a fictional lifecycle comparison with declared assumptions and a note on measurements still needed.
Public participation and accountability|Public decisions about technology need understandable evidence and meaningful participation. Expert knowledge informs but does not erase value choices. A consultation should allow objections to affect the decision, not merely record attendance.|A consultation occurs only after a decision is irreversible. What participation limitation exists?|People cannot influence the core choice. Earlier options, transparent criteria and a real response process are needed.|Design a fictional consultation plan with stakeholders, accessible information, alternatives and a documented response to concerns.
`)
add('GE6',"Art appreciation combines close observation, context and reasoned interpretation. Description identifies what is perceivable; interpretation explains possible meaning; evaluation states criteria. These processes can inform each other without collapsing into personal preference. Use original sketches or permitted examples and retain attribution; do not reproduce entire copyrighted artworks unnecessarily.",[ref('National Commission for Culture and the Arts','https://ncca.gov.ph/'),ip],`
Observation and description|Close observation records visible or audible features before interpreting them. Separate what is present from the emotion or story a viewer infers. Different interpretations can be reasoned when grounded in specific features.|A viewer writes the artist is angry because the picture is red. What inference needs support?|Color alone does not prove the artist's emotional state. Describe the red areas and consider context and alternative readings.|Describe an original sketch or permitted artwork using observable features, then list two interpretations and their evidence.
Elements and composition|Line, shape, color, texture, space and value contribute to visual composition. Balance, contrast and emphasis organize attention. These terms help explain an effect; they do not impose one universal formula for good art.|A small bright shape draws attention against a dark field. Which relation supports emphasis?|Contrast in value and color can create emphasis, along with placement and surrounding space.|Create two original compositions using the same shapes and explain how changed contrast and balance alter the viewing path.
Medium and process|A medium's properties shape possible marks, textures, scale and preservation. Digital and physical processes have different workflows. Technical description should identify what is known rather than infer a process solely from appearance.|A printed image resembles paint. Can the viewer confirm the original process from the print alone?|No. Reproduction can conceal material properties. Seek reliable process information and label uncertainty.|Compare two permitted examples in different media using process, material, audience and limitations of the reproduction.
Context and interpretation|Artwork exists within historical, social and personal contexts, but context should be evidenced. Do not reduce an entire community to one artist or one motif. Compare how context changes a plausible reading.|A symbol has different meanings in two settings. Why is one fixed interpretation inadequate?|Meaning depends on use, audience and historical context. A single dictionary-like label may erase the actual relationship.|Write a short contextual interpretation with source attribution, a close-observation paragraph and one alternative reading.
Critique and respectful evaluation|A critique states criteria, evidence and a reasoned judgment while distinguishing taste from argument. Feedback can address communication, technique or context without attacking the maker. Attribution and permissions remain part of responsible presentation.|A peer says I dislike it with no explanation. How can feedback become useful?|Name a criterion and specific feature, explain its effect and suggest a revision connected to the work's purpose.|Prepare a respectful critique of an original class-safe work and revise your own piece using one justified feedback point.
`)
add('GE7',"The Rizal course examines life, writings and historical context through evidence and interpretation. RA 1425 establishes the course's curricular basis, while the instructor confirms required editions and framing. Read the actual texts and distinguish narrator, fictional character, author and historian. These starter drafts do not invent quotations or treat a novel as a direct record of every historical event.",[ref('Rizal Law, RA 1425','https://lawphil.net/statutes/repacts/ra1956/ra_1425_1956.html')],`
Rizal studies and the curricular basis|Studying a national figure involves critical reading rather than unexamined praise. A law's curricular purpose differs from a historical argument about an author's ideas. Distinguish the source text, its edition and your interpretation.|Does a legal requirement to study an author prove every interpretation of that author is correct?|No. Interpretations still need textual and contextual evidence. The curricular basis does not settle every historical debate.|Read RA 1425 and draft questions for the course, separating what the law states from your learning goals.
Historical context and biography|A biography selects and connects events using evidence. Context includes institutions, social position, education and political conditions. Avoid explaining an entire life with one anecdote or assuming a later outcome was inevitable.|A biography infers motivation from one childhood story. What evidence limitation remains?|One anecdote cannot establish all later motives. Check provenance, corroboration and alternative explanations.|Build a sourced timeline of selected Rizal events using accessible reliable readings, recording uncertainty instead of filling unknown dates.
Reform, education and civic debate|Political writing addresses an audience and a historical problem. Analyze the argument, proposed change and supporting reasons rather than attributing every character's statement directly to the author. Contextual disagreement can be studied without requiring present-day endorsement.|A fictional character argues for a policy. Must that be the novelist's final position?|No. Narrative voice, irony, other characters and plot can complicate attribution. Read the surrounding work and context.|Analyze a permitted short passage from a required Rizal text with claim, audience, rhetorical method and exact edition/location.
Noli Me Tangere and social criticism|A novel can expose institutions and relationships through plot, characterization and irony. Interpret claims using actual episodes rather than invented quotations. Distinguish a character's perspective from a universal description of society.|A reader generalizes one character's behavior to every member of a social group. What interpretive problem occurs?|The claim exceeds the episode and erases variation. Support a narrower thematic reading with textual evidence and context.|Prepare a character/episode/theme table from the actual assigned Noli edition, including page or chapter references and an alternative reading.
El Filibusterismo and comparative reading|Comparative reading asks how themes and narrative choices change across works. Do not assume a sequel repeats the same argument unchanged. Examine means, consequences and competing voices through actual passages.|Two novels depict different responses to injustice. What makes a comparison defensible?|Use specific episodes, narrative context and a clear criterion rather than a slogan about either book. Acknowledge counterevidence.|Compare one theme across the assigned Noli and El Filibusterismo editions using two exact references, a counterinterpretation and a qualified conclusion.
`)
add('GE8',"The contemporary world is shaped by connected economic, political, cultural and environmental processes. Define the scale and time of a claim and identify whose experience it describes. Global patterns can coexist with local differences. These drafts teach analytical frameworks and fictional cases; current indicators and events must be checked from dated primary sources before classroom release.",[ref('United Nations Sustainable Development Goals','https://sdgs.un.org/goals')],`
Globalization and scale|Globalization describes intensified cross-border relationships, but different flows and groups experience them differently. Separate goods, information, capital, people and institutions. One international connection is evidence of a link, not proof of equal integration.|A local shop sells through an overseas platform. Which flows might be involved?|Goods, payments, information, platform rules and possibly logistics cross boundaries. Benefits and dependency may fall on different participants.|Map a fictional product's cross-border flows and identify the local actors, benefits, costs and evidence gaps.
Institutions and governance|States, international organizations, firms and civil society have distinct authority and responsibilities. Cooperation does not eliminate national or local institutions. Identify who can make, enforce or contest a rule.|A platform policy affects sellers in several countries. Is it automatically a national law?|No. Private platform rules differ from law, although both may influence behavior. Analyze the actual authority and applicable context.|Create a governance map for a fictional cross-border service, separating legal, contractual and technical controls.
Economy and interdependence|Trade and supply chains can specialize production and distribute risk. Aggregate gains do not mean every worker or community benefits equally. Trace inputs, labor, transport and market dependency before drawing a welfare conclusion.|A cheaper imported device benefits buyers but reduces local repair income. What should analysis include?|Consider distribution of gains and losses, alternatives, skills and longer-term effects rather than only the purchase price.|Analyze a fictional device supply chain with stakeholders, dependencies and two disruption scenarios.
Migration, identity and culture|Migration has varied causes and consequences for migrants and connected communities. Cultural exchange is not necessarily one-way replacement. Use specific contexts and avoid treating one story as representative of every migrant.|A news story depicts one migrant experience. Can it establish all migrants' motivations?|No. It offers one situated account; broader claims need suitable evidence and attention to diverse conditions.|Create a source-evaluation matrix for two migration accounts, distinguishing individual experience, statistics and interpretation.
Sustainability and shared problems|Cross-border problems need coordination while retaining local accountability. A goal requires a measure, baseline and responsible actors. Shared responsibility does not imply identical capacity or contribution from every community.|A city claims progress toward a global goal with no local baseline. What is missing?|A defined indicator, period, population and comparison are missing. Align the local measure with the goal and disclose limits.|Draft a fictional local action proposal linked to one UN goal, including evidence needs, access considerations and a review point.
`)
add('GE9',"Ethics examines what people ought to do and how reasons can justify or challenge a choice. Separate factual questions from value judgments while recognizing that both affect decisions. Apply frameworks as tools for analysis rather than formulas that erase disagreement. Use fictional dilemmas and respect private beliefs and the dignity of people affected.",[],`
Moral claims and reasons|A moral claim evaluates an action or obligation; a factual claim describes what is the case. An argument needs defensible premises and a conclusion that follows. Popularity alone does not establish moral justification.|Everyone does it is offered as a reason to falsify a record. What is the weakness?|The claim about common behavior does not justify deception or address its effects and duties. Even its factual premise needs evidence.|Analyze a fictional record-falsification dilemma, separating facts, values, assumptions and possible conclusions.
Consequences and affected people|Consequential reasoning evaluates outcomes and their distribution. Consider plausible alternatives, uncertainty and long-term effects rather than only an immediate benefit. Counting benefits does not permit ignoring whose harm is excluded from the calculation.|A shortcut saves staff time but exposes private learner data. What must consequence analysis include?|Include the learners' privacy harm, risk, trust and alternatives, not just the staff time saved.|Compare three fictional actions using stakeholders, benefits, harms, uncertainty and a reasoned recommendation.
Duties, rights and consistency|Duty-based analysis asks what obligations and rights constrain action. A rule should be examined for consistency and respect for people. Conflicting duties need explicit reasoning rather than selective application when convenient.|A manager promises confidentiality then shares records for convenience. Which duty is in tension?|The promise and privacy responsibility conflict with convenience; convenience alone does not erase the duty. Seek a legitimate, minimized alternative.|Write a fictional rights-and-duties analysis identifying conflicting obligations and the evidence needed to resolve them responsibly.
Virtue, character and care|Virtue ethics examines habits and practical judgment; care emphasizes relationships and responsiveness to particular needs. Neither requires favoritism or ignoring wider duties. Ask what a trustworthy person would notice and how a relationship can be supported fairly.|A friend asks an instructor to change a mark without evidence. Does care require agreement?|No. Care can offer explanation and support while preserving fair assessment and honest records.|Compare virtue and care perspectives on a fictional feedback dispute, proposing a compassionate action that preserves fairness.
Deliberation and ethical action|A defensible decision states facts, principles, options and limits, then takes responsibility for review. Different frameworks can reveal different concerns. Document why an option was selected and what new evidence would justify changing it.|An ethical review identifies a risk but assigns no action or owner. What remains incomplete?|The analysis has not become accountable action. Specify a responsible person, next step, safeguards and review point.|Prepare a fictional ethics memo combining two frameworks, a counterargument, practical action and a correction trigger.
`)
add('GEELECEM',"Entrepreneurial thinking tests whether a real need can be served responsibly and sustainably. A business idea is a hypothesis until evidence supports demand, delivery and cost assumptions. These drafts use fictional cases and arithmetic rather than promises of income. Distinguish interest, willingness to pay and actual transactions without inventing customers or sales.",[],`
Problems and value propositions|A value proposition connects a defined user problem to a useful outcome. Describe the current workaround and its cost before naming a product. A feature is valuable only in a context where someone needs and can use it.|A team proposes an app because classmates like apps. What evidence is missing?|The specific problem, target users, current behavior and reason to change are missing. General enthusiasm does not establish demand.|Draft a fictional problem/value proposition with user, task, alternative and an assumption that could disprove the idea.
Customer inquiry and assumptions|Inquiry should test assumptions rather than pressure people to praise an idea. Ask about actual recent behavior and constraints. Keep participant use ethical and authorized; a classroom plan can use fictional cases instead of real solicitation.|Would you buy our amazing app is asked in a survey. Why is it weak evidence?|The wording is leading and hypothetical intention may differ from purchase behavior. Explore the problem and existing decisions first.|Create a neutral five-question inquiry plan and an assumption/evidence table for a fictional service.
Business models and operations|A business model connects value, participants, delivery, revenue and cost. Map who pays and who receives the benefit; they may differ. Operational feasibility includes support, fulfillment and failure handling, not just acquiring users.|A free service earns advertising revenue. Who is the direct paying participant?|Advertisers pay under that model, while users supply attention or participation. Privacy and incentive conflicts need examination.|Draw a fictional business model with money, information and service flows, including one risk to user trust.
Costs and break-even reasoning|Separate fixed costs from per-unit variable costs. Contribution per unit is price minus variable cost under the simplified model. Break-even requires assumptions about demand, capacity and unchanged rates; it is not a guarantee of profit.|Fixed cost is 1,000, price 50 and variable cost 30 per item. Find simplified break-even quantity.|Contribution is 20 per item, so 50 items cover 1,000 in fixed cost. Other costs and demand are excluded assumptions.|Build a fictional break-even worksheet and test a lower-demand and higher-cost scenario, showing formulas and limits.
Experiments and responsible decisions|A small experiment should specify a hypothesis, measure, stopping rule and ethical boundary. Negative evidence can justify revising or stopping an idea. Do not convert clicks, likes or a trial signup into unverified revenue.|A prototype receives 100 visits and no purchases. What does the evidence establish?|It establishes recorded visits and no observed purchases in that setting, not profitable demand. Investigate audience, offer and measurement limits.|Design a fictional low-cost pilot with evidence criteria, privacy limits and explicit continue/revise/stop decisions; report no invented results.
`)
const nstp=ref('NSTP Act, RA 9163','https://lawphil.net/statutes/repacts/ra2002/ra_9163_2002.html')
add('NSTP1',"NSTP develops civic responsibility through the institution's actual authorized program component. These starter lessons prepare community inquiry, teamwork and responsible planning. They do not select a component for the learner, create service hours or authorize fieldwork. Use fictional cases until supervisors confirm participation, safety, permissions and the community's priorities.",[nstp,privacy],`
NSTP purpose and responsibilities|The NSTP framework includes distinct program components and institutional implementation. Read the actual enrollment and course requirements before planning work. A learning plan is not an attendance or completion record.|A learner assumes a drafted service plan counts as completed hours. Is that supported?|No. Planned work and verified participation are distinct. Follow the actual institution's recording and supervision requirements.|Prepare a course learning contract and a verified/pending requirements checklist using the actual syllabus.
Citizenship and community participation|Civic participation connects rights with responsibilities and attention to other people. A community is not merely a site for fulfilling a student requirement. Ask how people define their needs and how they can decline or revise a proposal.|Volunteers choose a project only because it is easy to photograph. What purpose is being neglected?|The community's expressed priorities and lasting benefit are neglected. Evidence and participation should guide the choice.|Analyze a fictional service case with community goals, student duties, power differences and a respectful consultation plan.
Needs assessment and evidence|Needs assessment separates observations, expressed priorities and assumptions. Use appropriate sources and minimal information. A survey or interview plan needs authorization and consent before real data collection.|A team assumes a neighborhood needs computer training without inquiry. What is the assumption?|The need, intended participants, access and useful skill level are unverified. Ask neutral questions through authorized consultation.|Draft a fictional needs-assessment matrix with questions, evidence sources, permissions and decisions the evidence would inform.
Teamwork and project planning|A service plan needs a goal, tasks, owners, resources, dependencies and review. Shared responsibility does not mean nobody is accountable. Include access, safety and continuity in the plan rather than adding them after scheduling.|Every member is assigned collect materials with no owner or deadline. What coordination gap appears?|Responsibility and timing are unclear. Assign a coordinating owner, specific outputs and a review point while retaining shared participation.|Build a fictional project task table with responsibilities, resource assumptions, dependencies and contingency decisions.
Responsible documentation|Documentation should record actual actions, participant permissions and observed results. Photos are not mandatory evidence when they compromise privacy or dignity. Outputs, outcomes and reflections are different and should be labelled accurately.|A report claims improved literacy after distributing leaflets. What is unverified?|Distribution is an output; improved literacy requires suitable outcome evidence. Do not infer it from a photograph or intention.|Create a fictional documentation template distinguishing planned work, verified participation, outputs, outcomes and unresolved follow-up.
`)
add('NSTP2',"NSTP 2 applies prior civic learning through a supervised, community-responsive project. Verify the actual program component, permissions and institutional requirements. These drafts use fictional plans and reports, never fabricated fieldwork or service hours. Implementation must respect the community's agency, data privacy and the supervisor's approved boundaries.",[nstp,privacy],`
Project readiness and continuity|A continuation project needs the prior needs evidence, community agreement and assigned responsibilities. Recheck assumptions that may have changed. A prior class's plan does not automatically authorize new participants or a changed activity.|A team copies last year's plan without contacting the partner. What remains unknown?|Current priorities, partner willingness, resources and permissions are unknown. Revalidate them before implementation.|Prepare a fictional readiness review with prior evidence, current confirmation needs and a go/revise/pending decision.
Objectives and measures|An objective specifies a bounded result, intended participants and timeframe. Measures should distinguish delivery from benefit. Choose feasible, respectful indicators and avoid collecting names when anonymous counts suffice.|An objective says help everyone. How can it be improved?|Define the actual target need, activity, feasible participants and evidence of success, while acknowledging limits.|Rewrite three fictional service objectives with output and outcome measures, data minimization and review ownership.
Implementation and adaptive teamwork|Implementation follows the approved plan while recording actual events and changes. When conditions differ, pause and seek the appropriate decision rather than improvising outside authority. Keep an issue log with responsibility and resolution.|The approved venue becomes unavailable. Can a team move to an unreviewed site without coordination?|Not automatically. Reassess permissions, access, safety and partner agreement through the supervisor's process.|Create a fictional implementation run sheet and issue log, including a venue-change decision and communication responsibilities.
Evaluation and participant feedback|Evaluation compares actual evidence with objectives and includes participant perspectives. Negative or mixed feedback is useful, not a reason to delete responses. Small or incomplete responses limit generalization.|Only three of twenty participants return feedback. Can the result represent everyone's view?|No. Report the response count and possible nonresponse bias; distinguish respondent feedback from the whole group's experience.|Design an ethical fictional feedback process and analysis table that preserves missing and negative responses visibly.
Turnover, reflection and verified reporting|A project ending needs responsible turnover, resource ownership and remaining obligations. Reflection examines effects, limits and learning. Verified hours and actual outcomes must come from real records and required supervision.|A report includes planned but unperformed activities as completed. What correction is needed?|Separate planned and actual work, correct totals and obtain the required verification. A polished narrative cannot substitute for genuine evidence.|Prepare a fictional final-report structure with actual-evidence fields, pending items, partner turnover and a learning reflection.
`)
const fitness=ref('WHO: Physical activity','https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/physical-activity')
const movementContext='Physical education combines knowledge, supervised practice and reflective evidence. Activities must follow the instructor\'s actual safety procedures and individual participation accommodations. These initial drafts support observation and planning; they prescribe no universal intensity or medical diagnosis. Record only actual participation, and use fictional examples where a physical demonstration has not occurred.'
add('PATHFIT1',movementContext,[fitness],`
Movement awareness and participation|Movement competency includes understanding body position, control and context. A checklist should describe observable actions rather than rank personal worth. Confirm space, equipment and instructor guidance before practical work.|A learner cannot perform a planned movement comfortably. Should the record claim successful completion?|No. Record the actual observation and seek the instructor's appropriate alternative. Do not infer a medical condition or force a demonstration.|Prepare a supervised-session readiness checklist and a fictional observation log with performed, modified and unperformed states.
Balance and base of support|Balance concerns control of body position relative to support. Stance, surface and task alter difficulty. Observation describes what happened without diagnosing the cause. Practice progression belongs under qualified instruction.|A narrower support area makes a task harder for one participant. Does that prove a disorder?|No. Task conditions, experience and many other factors can affect performance. Record the context and seek appropriate guidance.|Draw and compare three support arrangements on paper, predicting difficulty and listing observations needed during an instructor-led demonstration.
Locomotor and nonlocomotor patterns|Locomotor patterns move the body through space; nonlocomotor patterns occur largely around a position. Clear task descriptions help learners distinguish action, direction and control. Technique feedback should be specific and respectful.|Compare walking across a room with bending in place. Which is locomotor?|Walking changes location and is locomotor; bending in place is nonlocomotor under this classification.|Create a movement-classification chart from instructor-approved examples and write observable cues without prescribing an unsupervised drill.
Coordination and task progression|Coordination organizes actions for a goal. A progression changes one relevant condition at a time so feedback remains understandable. Increasing speed and complexity together can obscure which change affected control.|A task becomes faster and adds equipment simultaneously. What comparison becomes difficult?|It is difficult to isolate whether speed, equipment or their combination affected performance. State the changed conditions separately.|Design a paper progression for an instructor-selected skill with one changed condition per stage and a clear observation criterion.
Reflection and evidence of progress|Progress compares the same meaningful task under documented conditions. Attendance, confidence and demonstrated competency are distinct. A learner can reflect on effort and needs without inventing improvement or disclosing private health information.|A learner attended twice but has no recorded skill observation. What can the report claim?|It can report actual attendance if verified, not demonstrated skill improvement. Identify the missing assessment evidence.|Create a personal or fictional progress portfolio structure with conditions, observations, feedback and next instructor-approved practice.
`)
add('PATHFIT2',movementContext,[fitness],`
Fitness concepts and personal context|Fitness includes several capacities and is specific to goals and circumstances. A single performance does not describe a person's overall health. Course planning must use the actual instructor's assessment and accommodation process.|A person completes one task quickly. Does that prove every dimension of fitness is high?|No. Different capacities and contexts require different evidence; one result cannot establish an overall health judgment.|Create a conceptual map of fitness dimensions and the evidence a qualified instructor would use, without collecting private health data.
Planning variables and specificity|Frequency, intensity, time and type describe exercise planning variables. Appropriate choices depend on the participant and goal. General principles are not individualized prescriptions; progression should follow the instructor's guidance.|Two learners have different experience and accommodations. Must one fixed plan suit both?|No. The same learning objective can require different appropriate participation paths and instructor-approved adjustments.|Analyze a fictional exercise plan by identifying its variables, assumptions and questions requiring instructor guidance.
Monitoring effort and observations|A training log records activity, duration, context and actual response. It supports reflection but is not a diagnosis. Do not convert an unperformed session into a zero-performance score or imply safety from a single number.|A session was missed. Should its log contain an invented effort rating?|No. Mark it unperformed or missing and state what can be verified. An effort rating requires actual participation.|Design a privacy-conscious session log and interpret fictional completed, modified and missed sessions distinctly.
Progression, recovery and consistency|Adaptation depends on appropriate practice and recovery over time. More work is not automatically better. Changing several variables at once makes cause and effect harder to interpret. The actual instructor sets practical limits and modifications.|A learner increases every variable after one successful session. What planning concern arises?|The change lacks a controlled progression and may exceed the appropriate plan. Review with the instructor rather than infer readiness from one success.|Create a fictional plan-review worksheet comparing baseline, one proposed change, recovery considerations and instructor approval.
Evaluating a fitness claim|Fitness claims should state population, method, outcome and limitations. Testimonials and before/after images do not isolate causes. Evaluate evidence without diagnosing classmates or promising guaranteed outcomes.|An advertisement guarantees identical results for every participant. What evidence problem exists?|Individual context and response vary; a universal guarantee exceeds what a testimonial can establish. Examine methods and limitations.|Audit a public fitness claim and write a source-based, qualified interpretation with questions for an appropriate instructor.
`)
add('PATHFIT3',movementContext,[fitness],`
Sport, rules and fair participation|Sport combines goals, rules, roles and agreed conduct. The instructor selects the actual sport and applicable rule set. Fair play includes truthful scoring and respect for officials and opponents, not merely avoiding penalties.|Two teams use different scoring rules. What must be agreed before play?|Use one stated rule set, clarify scoring and responsibilities, and ensure participants understand the conditions.|Create a fictional sport-orientation sheet with rules source, roles, participation accommodations and dispute procedure.
Skill observation and feedback|A skill observation should identify the task, relevant cues and actual result. Feedback is more useful when specific and actionable. One attempt cannot establish a permanent ability ranking.|A coach says bad technique with no detail. What would improve the feedback?|Name an observable action, its relation to the task and one appropriate next cue, respecting the learner.|Develop an instructor-reviewed observation rubric for the selected sport, using clear descriptions and no fabricated performances.
Space, tactics and decisions|Tactics connect available space, teammates, opponents and the goal. A decision can be reasonable even when execution fails. Analyze the options and information available at the time rather than only the final score.|A safe pass is dropped by a teammate. Was the passing decision necessarily wrong?|No. Evaluate options, pressure and expected opportunity separately from execution outcome.|Draw three fictional game situations and compare available decisions using space, risk, support and the sport's actual rules.
Team communication and inclusion|Teams coordinate through agreed signals, roles and respectful feedback. Communication should support participation rather than shame a member. Adapt roles to the instructor's objectives and accommodations without inventing competence.|A team ignores a new participant during practice. What learning opportunity is lost?|The participant loses meaningful involvement and the team loses coordination practice. Define inclusive roles and clear communication.|Write a fictional team communication protocol with role rotation, constructive feedback and an appropriate conflict process.
Performance evidence and sportsmanship|A game record should preserve actual events and scoring rules. Statistics need context such as attempts and playing opportunity. A win does not establish perfect skill or ethical conduct.|One player scores twice from ten attempts and another twice from two. Are raw totals a complete comparison?|No. Attempts and context differ; equal totals do not describe equal rates or opportunities.|Analyze a fictional game sheet using totals and denominators, then reflect on sportsmanship and limitations of the statistics.
`)
add('PATHFIT4',movementContext,[ref('WHO: Drowning prevention','https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/drowning')],`
Aquatic safety and authorized instruction|Aquatic participation requires the institution's actual qualified supervision, venue procedures and individual readiness process. These documents are not a substitute for in-person instruction. Never enter water to perform an unsupervised courseware exercise.|A learner wants to practice alone before the instructor arrives. What should the course plan require?|Wait for the authorized supervised setting and follow its safety procedures. Written confidence does not establish water competency.|Prepare a dry-land orientation checklist identifying supervisor, venue rules, emergency contacts and participation accommodations.
Hazards, supervision and boundaries|Water depth, visibility, conditions and supervision affect risk. A person can be in difficulty without dramatic shouting. Observation and emergency response must follow trained personnel and venue procedures rather than improvised rescue attempts.|A pool appears calm but has no authorized supervisor present. Does calm water establish a safe class setting?|No. Required supervision and procedures remain essential. Appearance alone does not establish readiness for participation.|Create a fictional hazard map and a venue-rule question list for the instructor, with no water-entry activity.
Buoyancy and water concepts|Buoyancy relates upward fluid force to displaced fluid; body position and conditions affect observed behavior. Understanding a concept does not prove an individual can float safely. Practical demonstrations require the actual instructor.|Does knowing the definition of buoyancy establish swimming competence?|No. Conceptual knowledge and supervised practical competency are distinct forms of evidence.|Draw a conceptual buoyancy diagram and prepare observation questions for an instructor-led demonstration, leaving unobserved performance ungraded.
Skill sequences and observation|Aquatic skill instruction uses supervised progressions and individual feedback. A course document should identify what will be observed and by whom without prescribing an unsupervised sequence. Learners may need different approved participation paths.|A checklist is completed from reading alone. Can it certify an aquatic skill?|No. Reading shows access to information, not an observed practical skill. Qualification requires the actual authorized assessment.|Design a blank instructor observation sheet with task, conditions, feedback and verified/pending states, without entering invented skill results.
Emergency awareness and reflection|Emergency awareness includes knowing how to alert trained responders and follow the venue's procedures. Do not improvise an in-water rescue. Reflection can identify readiness questions and learning needs without claiming training or certification not received.|A course reader displays emergency guidance. Does that certify the learner as a lifeguard?|No. Certification and practical rescue competence require their actual authorized training and assessment.|Create a dry-land emergency-communication map and a reflective learning checklist, clearly distinguishing information reviewed from training actually completed.
`)
add('FL',"The recorded course lists Korean, Mandarin or Nihongo without a selected language track. These drafts explicitly use a proposed Japanese starter track from the Japan Foundation; the instructor must confirm it or replace it before approval. Romanization is a temporary aid, not a complete pronunciation guide. Use the linked original audio for listening; this text does not create spoken-performance evidence.",[ref('Japan Foundation: Irodori Starter','https://www.irodori.jpf.go.jp/en/starter/00.html')],`
Track orientation and Japanese writing|Japanese uses hiragana, katakana and kanji in different roles; romanization represents sounds with Latin letters. A language track must be confirmed for this offering. Recognizing a script is different from understanding every word or producing accurate speech.|Does reading romanized Japanese establish that a learner can read kanji?|No. Romanization uses a different representation and does not establish kanji recognition or meaning.|Prepare a proposed Japanese-track learning contract and script-recognition chart using permitted Irodori examples, pending instructor confirmation.
Greetings and social context|Greetings depend on time, relationship and situation. Konnichiwa is a daytime greeting, while polite morning and evening greetings differ. Use the linked native audio and context rather than assuming a written approximation captures pronunciation.|Is one memorized greeting appropriate in every time and relationship context?|No. Match the expression to context and level of politeness; verify its use in the assigned source and audio.|Create a greeting/context table from the official starter lesson, with source location and an instructor-reviewed listening practice plan.
Self-introduction and names|A simple introduction can identify a name with watashi wa followed by the name and desu in a suitable beginner context. A conversation can omit information already clear. Do not translate a person's identity or presume nationality from appearance.|A model introduction uses a fictional name. Must learners disclose private personal details to practice?|No. A fictional identity can meet the communication objective without unnecessary disclosure.|Draft a short fictional introduction from the assigned source model, identify the name slot and record pronunciation practice only if actually performed.
Numbers and practical listening|Number words support times, quantities and prices, but counters and context can change forms. Begin with the actual lesson's limited expressions. Listening recognition and written recall are distinct; do not claim one from the other.|A learner recognizes a written numeral but misses it in audio. What evidence differs?|Written recognition does not establish listening comprehension. Practice with the actual source audio and record observed responses separately.|Create a five-item fictional number-listening worksheet using official audio timestamps and an instructor-checked answer key before classroom use.
Requests, clarification and review|A beginner can communicate more responsibly by requesting repetition and acknowledging limits. Politeness and context matter. A memorized sentence should be understood in purpose, not used as a universal phrase detached from the situation.|A learner does not understand a spoken instruction. What communication strategy is preferable to pretending comprehension?|Use the assigned polite clarification expression or ask the instructor for repetition; confirm the intended action rather than guessing.|Prepare a short fictional dialogue with greeting, introduction and clarification, linked to official lesson examples and pending pronunciation review.
`)
